longitudinal alterations in functional neural networks associated with cognitive control in cocaine use disorder (CUD). Methods: Secondary analysis was performed on data from 44 individuals who participated in three randomized clinical trials for CUD and completed an fMRI Stroop task both at baseline and post-treatment. Independent component analysis (ICA) was performed to assess changes in functional network engagement and investigate associations with cocaine-use behaviors. Mixed linear models were performed to test for longitudinal effects on network engagement and relationships with baseline patterns of cocaine use (i.e., past-month frequency and lifetime years of use) and periods of abstinence/use between scans (i.e., percent negative urine toxicology and maximum days of contiguous abstinence). Results: Six functional networks were identified as being related to cognitive control and/or exhibiting changes in engagement following treatment. Results indicated that engagement of amygdala-striatal, middle frontal and right-frontoparietal networks were reduced over time in CUD. Less change in the amygdala-striatal network was associated with greater lifetime years of cocaine use. Additional analyses revealed that negative toxicology results and achievement of continuous abstinence were associated with greater engagement of the right-frontoparietal network. Conclusions: Neural systems that underlie cognitive control may change over time in individuals with CUD. A longer history of cocaine-use may hinder changes in network activity, potentially impeding recovery. Keywords: cocaine, imaging, Treatment, fMRI, Independent Component Analysis, longitudinal Introduction Examining neural correlates of cognitive-control impairments using functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) has been an important step toward understanding neurobiological processes associated with substance-use disorders (SUDs) ( Volkow et al., 2003 ). Alterations in cognitive control are implicated in addictive processes including attentional bias, impaired decision-making, deficient response inhibition, and compulsive maladaptive behaviors ( Goldstein and Volkow, 2011 ; Zilverstand et al., 2018 ). Neurobiological models of addictions posit that functional impairments in brain regions and networks involved in cognitive control, coupled with alterations in motivational, emotional, and salience attribution circuitry, contribute to the maintenance and persistence of addictive behaviors ( Koob and Volkow, 2016 ; Volkow et al., 2016 ; Volkow et al., 2013 ). Cognitive-control related brain activity in cocaine- and stimulant-use disorders, particularly in regions of the prefrontal cortex, has been shown to predict initiation of substance use in younger individuals, classify individuals with a current SUD, and predict relapse ( Clark et al., 2014 ; Mahmood et al., 2013 ; Sakoglu et al., 2019 ). Limited evidence suggests cognitive functioning may improve over time with reductions in drug use ( Vonmoos et al., 2014 ); however, less is known regarding the potential recovery of associated brain systems with changes in cocaine use. Cross-sectional fMRI studies of abstinence-related changes in cognitive-control broadly suggest partial recovery of functioning in individuals with cocaine-use disorder (CUD). People with former cocaine use (6+ months abstinent) display greater error-related activity in prefrontal and parietal regions relative to those with current use during performance of a response inhibition task ( Castelluccio et al., 2014 ). Similarly, among individuals with CUD those with both recent (1–5 weeks) and longer-term (40–102 weeks) abstinence display greater prefrontal activity relative to healthy individuals during both successful response inhibition and commission errors ( Connolly et al., 2012a ). These data suggest some recovery of cognitive-control functioning may be observed following a period of abstinence in CUD, although not always. By comparison, direct longitudinal studies of treatment-seeking CUD broadly suggest short-term reductions in neural activations underlying of cognitive control. At a 6-month follow-up relative to baseline, individuals that had undergone treatment for CUD displayed reduced cognitive-control-related activity in prefrontal regions ( Moeller et al., 2012 ). In a series of preliminary longitudinal studies, we have reported reductions in regional brain activity during performance of a cognitive control (Stroop) task following treatment for CUD. Following an 8-week study of a cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT), brain activity was reduced relative to baseline across a range of cognitive-control related regions including the anterior cingulate, right inferior frontal gyrus, right dorsolateral prefrontal cortex, thalamus and midbrain in people with SUDs, most of whom had CUD ( DeVito et al., 2012 ). Following a 12-week clinical trial of disulfiram (DSF), contingency management (CM), and cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT), reduced brain activity in the cingulate, thalamus, and precuneus was observed in patients with CUD ( DeVito et al., 2017 ). Finally, following an 8-week clinical trial of CBT for CUD in individuals with a co-occurring opioid use disorder, reduced cognitive-control-related activity was again observed in the prefrontal cortex, thalamus, and midbrain regions ( DeVito et al., 2019 ). Together, these longitudinal studies indicate reliable reductions in cognitive-control-related regional brain activity following treatment in individuals with CUD. However, the extent to which these regional alterations over time in CUD reflect changes in the activity of distinct functional networks remains unexplored. Independent component analysis (ICA) is a data-driven computational technique that allows for the investigation of temporally coherent and spatially distributed functional brain networks ( Calhoun et al., 2001 ). ICA of fMRI has identified numerous functional networks that are present during resting-state and task performance, and engagement of these networks has been linked to multiple distinct cognitive domains (e.g., attention, inhibition, and motivation) ( Laird et al., 2011a ; Smith and Nichols, 2009 ). Changes in the engagement patterns of functional networks have been associated with substance use and learning. For example, changes in connectivity between salience, executive-function, and default-mode networks were observed within individuals with tobacco-use disorder following smoking relative to a nicotine-deprived baseline ( Ding and Lee, 2013 ). Changes in network engagement have also been linked to skills training, as seen in the strengthening of a resting-state motor network after four weeks of practicing a finger-tapping task ( Ma et al., 2011 ). Examining potential alterations in distinct functional networks over time in CUD may provide insight into the recovery of dissociable functional processes and inform treatment-development strategies. Our prior investigation of functional network activity in treatment-seeking CUD participants identified five cognitive-control-related networks that did not display engagement differences relative to a healthy control sample at baseline; however, engagement of a subcortical network and a ventral-prefrontal network before treatment were associated with abstinence during treatment ( Worhunsky et al., 2013 ). In the current study, we aimed to extend this investigation by examining longitudinal changes in functional network activity in a secondary analysis of 44 treatment-seeking individuals with CUD included in three prior reports ( DeVito et al., 2017 ; DeVito et al., 2019 ; DeVito et al., 2012 ). Consistent with prior Stroop-related findings of reduced regional activity at post-treatment ( DeVito et al., 2017 ; DeVito et al., 2019 ; DeVito et al., 2012 ), and a positive association between baseline subcortical network engagement and greater abstinence during CUD treatment ( Worhunsky et al., 2013 ), we hypothesized that engagement of cortical executive-control and subcortical motivation networks would be reduced at post-treatment scans relative to baseline. Further, we hypothesized that greater reductions in network engagement would be associated with abstinence-related outcomes (i.e., percent cocaine-negative urine toxicologies and maximum duration of continuous cocaine abstinence during treatment). Methods Participants and cocaine-use measures Participants were 44 treatment-seeking individuals with a primary diagnosis of CUD (age=39.9 years, SD= 8.4; 20 female) enrolled in randomized clinical trials who completed fMRI procedures at the beginning of and following treatment ( DeVito et al., 2017 ; DeVito et al., 2019 ; DeVito et al., 2012 ). Briefly, clinical trials included two separate 8-week trials of a computer-based training for CBT (CBT4CBT) relative to treatment-as-usual, and a 12-week trial of disulfiram and/or contingency management in augmenting therapist-administered CBT, with additional information about the clinical trials described previously ( Carroll et al., 2008 ; Carroll et al., 2009 ; Carroll, 2014 ; Carroll et al., 2016 ). Original data collection across the three clinical trials was conducted between 2007 and 2009. In brief, all participants met DSM criteria for CUD, had no other major co-occurring psychiatric disorder (e.g., schizophrenia, bipolar, etc) and had no contraindications for MRI procedures (e.g., were claustrophobic, had implanted metallic devices, etc). One study allowed opioid use disorder (participants were methadone-maintained) and all studies permitted tobacco use. Ten participants were receiving methadone maintenance therapy (MMT) for a co-occurring opioid use disorder ( DeVito et al., 2019 ). Cocaine-use measures included recent cocaine use frequency at baseline (days of use in the month prior to treatment), lifetime cocaine use chronicity (total years of use), percentage of cocaine-negative urine samples submitted during treatment, and the maximum duration of self-reported continuous abstinence assessed through self-reports using the timeline follow-back ( Sobell and Sobell, 1992 ). We also collected information on whether or not participants stayed in contact throughout their treatment regimen, and participants were categorized as “completers” if they did not drop out of their specific treatment regimen. Stroop fMRI task and imaging processing Participants completed six runs of an event-related Stroop color-word interference task ( Leung et al., 2000 ) during fMRI using a Siemens Trio 3T MR system (Siemens AG, Erlangen, Germany). Details of the Stroop task and acquisition parameters are provided in Supplementary Materials . For purposes of definitions, the Stroop “interference” effect is defined as the difference in response between incongruent and congruent conditions; similarly, interference engagement refers to the difference between engagement in response to incongruent and congruent conditions. Functional images were spatially processed using SPM12 (Wellcome Trust Centre for Neuroimaging, London, UK), including motion correction, registration to standard space, and smoothing with a 6mm FWHM Gaussian kernel. Individuals completed two scanning sessions at an average of 89.5 (SD=30.0) days apart. Independent component analysis (ICA) and network selection ICA was performed on the fMRI time series using the Group ICA Toolbox (GIFT v4.0c; http://trendscenter.org/software/gift ). The spatially processed data from all participants at both timepoints was concatenated into a single group and initially reduced through a principal component analysis. As primary hypotheses regarded activity of large-scale functional networks, 40 independent components ( Abou-Elseoud et al., 2010 ) were extracted from the aggregate dataset and iterated 20 times using ICASSO to assess stability and repeatability of extracted components ( Himberg and Hyvarinen, 2003 ). Component time courses and spatial source maps for each subject were back-reconstructed for each timepoint from the group aggregate, and signal amplitudes were scaled to percent signal change to facilitate between-subject comparisons. Of the 40 extracted components, 23 were excluded from analyses as likely artefactual sources through spectral analysis and visual inspection ( Allen et al., 2011 ). Task-related engagement of the remaining 17 components was assessed using multiple regression analysis of component time courses with modeled BOLD responses to incongruent and congruent stimuli (similar to GLM approaches typically used to identify whole-brain task-related activity), following previous methods ( Worhunsky et al., 2013 ). To identify networks of interest, the resulting β-weights were entered into separate 2 (condition; incongruent, congruent) × 2 (time; baseline, post-treatment) repeated-measures models to identify components displaying a condition-by-time interaction or main effects of time or condition using a false-discovery-rate-corrected (FDR-corrected) threshold of p FDR <0.001. As there were no condition-by-time interactions or main effects of time or condition that survived initial thresholds, these effects were explored at a lower threshold of p FDR <0.05. This process identified a total of six networks displaying a significant main or interactive effect in repeated measure analysis of variance (RM-ANOVAs): one network displaying a condition-by-time interaction, no networks displaying a main effect of time, and five functional networks displaying a main effect of condition. Statistical analyses Subsequent analyses to investigate potential influences of baseline cocaine use and abstinence between scans were performed on the six identified networks. All analyses were performed using SPSS v26.0 (IBM Corp., Armonk, NY). β-weights were entered into separate 2 (condition; incongruent, congruent) × 2 (time; baseline, post-treatment) repeated-measures models, with cocaine use measures included as covariates. These measures included baseline cocaine use and within-treatment abstinence measures. Baseline cocaine-use measures of interest included recent use (days of use in the month prior to treatment) and lifetime cocaine use (total years of use). Within-treatment abstinence measures of interest included percent cocaine-negative urine toxicology and maximum continuous self-reported abstinence collected as part of the clinical trials. Analyses to examine relationships between cocaine-use measures and network engagement were conducted using separate condition-by-time repeated-measure models with the inclusion of covariates of cocaine-use measures of interest. Interactions between covariates and effects of condition, time, or the condition-by-time interaction on network engagement were considered significant at uncorrected p<0.05. When cocaine-use covariates significantly (uncorrected p<0.05) contributed to the model, post-hoc analyses using Pearson correlations were conducted to determine the nature of relationships between cocaine-use measures and network engagement. Additional exploratory analyses were performed to examine potential influences of concurrent methadone maintenance and between-group factors of treatment-received. Treatment received was categorized as any CBT alone (i.e., CBT4CBT or therapist-administered CBT; N=15), CBT+ (i.e., with DSF and/or CM; N=19), or TAU (N=10), and treatment completion (N=29) vs. drop out (N=15). For these analyses, β-weights were entered into separate 2 (condition; incongruent, congruent) × 2 (time; baseline, post-treatment) repeated-measures models, with MMT and treatment received included as additional between-group variables. Results Participants and cocaine-use outcomes Participant characteristics and cocaine-use outcomes for the 44 individuals with CUD are summarized in Table 1 and provided by treatment categories (CBT, CBT+, TAU) in Supplemental Table S1 . Cocaine-use measures (i.e., baseline use frequency, lifetime chronicity, negative urine toxicology and maximum days of continuous abstinence) were not associated with age, did not differ by gender or education level, and did not differ between treatment categories (p-values >.38). Similarly, age, gender, ethnicity/race, years of education and years of cocaine use and did not differ between treatment categories (p-values >.16). Correlations were performed between baseline measures of cocaine use and cocaine-use outcomes. Years of use before treatment did not correlate significantly with treatment measures (percent negative urines (r=.17, p=.26) or with maximum days abstinent (r=.20, p=.18)). Days of use before treatment did not correlate significantly with maximum days reported abstinence (r=.25, p=.14) but did correlate with percent negative urines (r=.42, p=.01). Percent negative urines and maximum days abstinent were correlated (r=.74, p<.001). Table 1. Participant characteristics and cocaine use measures at baseline and post-treatment Variable Value Demographics Age, years ± SD 39.9 ±8.4 Female gender N, % 20, 45.5 High-school education or higher N, % 36, 81.8 Methadone maintenance, N, % 10, 22.7 Baseline measures Lifetime cocaine use, years ±SD 10.2 ±6.8 Past-month cocaine use, days ±SD 12.6 ±9.1 Last cocaine use before MRI a , days ±SD 6.6 ±6.9 Outcome measures Cocaine-negative urine, % ±SD 47.4 ±39.8 Maximum continuous cocaine abstinence, days ±SD 22.6 ±23.1 Days between MRI scans, days ±SD 89.5 ±30.0 Last cocaine use before MRI a , days ±SD 34.7 ±37.6 Open in a new tab a Last cocaine use prior to scanning reflects available data at baseline (N=35) and post-treatment (N=33). Functional networks during Stroop performance The series of 2 (condition; incongruent, congruent) by 2 (time; baseline, post-treatment) RM-ANOVA performed on network engagement beta-weights identified six functional networks of interest that displayed significant main or interaction effects ( Figure 1 ; details of regional composition are provided in Supplementary Table S2 ). Networks displaying a main effect of condition on engagement included amygdala-striatal, medial prefrontal, middle frontal, and right and left frontoparietal networks (p-values <0.001). Post-hoc, paired t-tests revealed that engagement of all five networks was greater for incongruent stimuli relative to congruent stimuli during both baseline and post-treatment scans (p-values<.002; Figure 2 ). The default-mode network displayed a condition-by-time interaction (F 1,42 =10.52, p=.002), with greater congruent-related disengagement at baseline and greater incongruent-related engagement at post-treatment, though pairwise tests between conditions within each time point did not achieve significance (p-values>.12; Figure 2 ). The amygdala-striatal network also showed a condition-by-time interaction (F 1,42 =4.84, p=.03); however, this effect did not survive corrections for multiple comparisons. No networks displayed a significant effect of time (p-values> 0.2). Identified networks were largely consistent with established intrinsic functional connectivity networks previously associated with a range of executive functions including cognitive control and inhibitory processing ( Laird et al., 2011a , b ). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Spatial maps of the six functional networks associated with Stroop interference processing during baseline scans: AMST, amygdala-striatal; MedF, medial prefrontal; MidF, middle frontal; RFP, right frontoparietal; LFP left frontoparietal; DMN, default-mode network. Slices displayed are results from one-sample t-test (N=44) of the spatial loadings at family-wise-error corrected p FWE <0.0001 and a cluster-extent threshold of k>100. Regions displayed represent sources of concurrent positive signal change. Left hemisphere on left. Numbers next to the brain slices indicate the z-coordinates of slices in MNI space used to show components. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Functional network engagement associated with incongruent and congruent color-word Stroop stimuli at baseline and post-treatment in CUD. Five networks displayed a main effect of condition (i.e., significant interference engagement) including amygdala-striatal, medial prefrontal, middle frontal, and right and left frontoparietal networks (*p-values <0.001). The default-mode network showed a condition-by-time interaction (i.e., a significant change in interference engagement at post-treatment relative to baseline) ( ++ p<0.01). The amygdala-striatal network also showed a condition-by-time interaction, although this effect did not survive corrections for multiple comparisons ( + p<0.05). Network engagement and baseline cocaine use Relationships between network engagement and baseline cocaine use were investigated by including covariates (lifetime years and past-month use) separately in 2-by-2 RM-ANOVA models for each network. With years of use included as a covariate, main effects of condition survived (p values <.001), the DMN condition-by-time interaction was no longer significant (F 1,42 =3.80, p=0.058), and no main effects of time were detected (p values > .1). However, the model investigating amygdala-striatal engagement and years of cocaine use displayed a condition-by-time interaction (F 1,42 =12.23, p=0.001) and a three-way interaction with years of use (F 1,42 =6.98 p=.012). Follow-up analyses on the condition-by-time interaction revealed reduced interference-related engagement (i.e., incongruent vs congruent) at the post-treatment visit relative to baseline. Follow-up analyses on the three-way interaction revealed that longer use histories were associated with a reduced condition-by-time effect (i.e., less change in interference engagement at post-treatment relative to baseline) (r=.33, p=.012) ( Figure 3A ).” Figure 3. Open in a new tab Scatter plots illustrating associations between covariates of interest and network engagement indicated by the RM-ANOVAs. 3A) Longer cocaine-use histories were associated with less reduction in interference engagement (i.e., incongruent – congruent) of the amygdala-striatal network at post-treatment relative to baseline. 3B) Similarly, longer use histories were associated with reduced interference engagement on average at both post-treatment and baseline scans (i.e., mean interference of both timepoints). 3C) A higher percentage of cocaine-negative urine toxicology results between scans was associated with greater average engagement (i.e., within-scan mean of incongruent and congruent engagement) of the amygdala-striatal network at post-treatment relative to baseline. 3D) Similarly, achieving a longer duration of continuous abstinence between scans was associated with greater average engagement of the right-frontoparietal network at post-treatment relative to baseline. The model investigating medial-prefrontal engagement with years of use included similarly revealed a condition-by-time interaction on engagement that was significant for the medial-prefrontal network (F 1,42 = 4.67, p=0.036), with reduced interference engagement seen at post-treatment. There was no three-way interaction with condition, time, and years of use (p=.11). Further, an exploratory probe was performed to examine the potential direction of the non-significant relationship between years of use and change in engagement of the medial prefrontal network. Findings suggested increased years of use was marginally associated with less change in engagement in this network, though correlations did not reach significance (r=.24, p=.11) ( Supplemental Figure S1A ). Years of use was not correlated with medial prefrontal network engagement at baseline or post-treatment (p-values >.17). For the model investigating right frontoparietal engagement with years of use included, there was an interaction of years of use and condition (F 1,42 =7.12, p=.011). Post-hoc analyses indicated that on average across both sessions, longer use-histories were associated with reduced interference-related engagement (r=−.38, p=.011) ( Figure 3B ). In a series of RM-ANOVA models that included past-month use as a covariate, main effects of condition survived (p values <.01) and there were no main effects of time. The DMN condition-by-time interaction was no longer significant (F 1,42 =2.71, p=0.11) and none of the other five network displayed a condition-by-time interaction (p-values >.07). Past-month use of cocaine did not interact with main effects of condition or time, or display a three-way interaction, (p-values>.1). Additional exploratory correlations were examined between baseline cocaine use measures (years and months of use) and interference engagement at baseline and at post-treatment separately. More years of use was associated with reduced interference engagement at baseline in the amygdala-striatal network (r=−.35, p=.018). There were no other associations with years of use (all p values >.1). There were no associations between days of use prior to treatment and interference engagement in any network at baseline or post-treatment (all p values >.2). Network associations with cocaine-use outcomes Relationships between network engagement and cocaine-use outcomes were investigated by including covariates (cocaine-negative urines and maximum days abstinent) separately in 2-by-2 RM-ANOVA models for each network. When examining each network with maximum days abstinent as a covariate in the condition × time ANOVAs, the main effect of condition survived for all networks (all p values <.001) with the exception of the DMN (p=.89). A main effect of time was seen for the right-frontoparietal network (p=.011), but no other networks showed a main effect of time (all p values >.07). The model investigating amygdala-striatal engagement with maximum days abstinent included revealed a condition-by-time interaction on engagement (F 1,42 =4.68, p=0.036), with reduced interference engagement seen at post-treatment. No other network showed a condition × time interaction (p values > .12). The model investigating right-frontoparietal engagement revealed an interaction of time and maximum days abstinent, indicating that longer durations of maximum continuous cocaine abstinence were associated with greater engagement of the right-frontoparietal network (F 1,42 =8.65, r=.41, p=0.005). When examining each network with percent negative urines as a covariate in the condition × time ANOVAs, the main effect of condition survived for all networks (all p values > .001) with the exception of marginal difference in the DMN (p=.06). A main effect of time was seen for the right-frontoparietal network (p=.024) and in the amygdala-striatal network (p=.03), and other networks showed no main effect of time (all p values >.06). The model investigating amygdala-striatal engagement with percent negative urines included revealed an interaction between time and percent negative urines on engagement (F 1,42 =4.32, r =.31, p=0.044 ( Figure 3C ), indicating that greater percentage of cocaine-negative urine samples submitted during treatment was associated with greater average engagement (i.e., incongruent and congruent) at post-treatment relative to baseline for the amygdala-striatal network. Similarly, the model investigating right-frontoparietal network engagement revealed an interaction between time and percent negative urines on engagement (F 1,42 =5.67, r=.4, p=0.022; Figure 3D ), indicating that greater percentage of cocaine-negative urine samples submitted between scans was associated with greater average engagement (i.e., incongruent and congruent) at post-treatment relative to baseline for the right-frontoparietal network. The model investigating middle-frontal engagement with percent negative urines included revealed a significant condition-by-time interaction on engagement (F 1,42 =4.82, p=0.034) in this network, with reduced interference engagement seen at post-treatment. There was a marginal three-way interaction (p=.08). An exploratory probe was performed to examine the potential direction of the non-significant relationship between cocaine-negative urines and change in engagement of the middle frontal network. Findings suggested a positive relationship, implying that more cocaine-negative urines was marginally associated with less change in engagement in this network, though correlations did not reach significance (r=.25, p=.08) ( Supplementary Figure S1B ). Additional factors relating to cocaine-use outcomes and network engagement Additional factors that may have influenced patterns of network engagement over time were explored, including concurrent MMT, the time between scans, treatments received, and whether treatment was completed. Concurrent MMT was associated with a greater reduction in average engagement (F 1,42 =5.19, p=0.028) and reduced interference engagement (F 1,42 =5.13, p=0.029) of the right-frontoparietal network. There were no differences in cocaine-use outcomes between treatment types (p-values>.33). Individuals receiving treatment-as-usual displayed greater reductions in interference engagement of the right-frontoparietal network relative to the other active treatment groups (F 2,41 =3.98, p=0.026). Additionally, we explored whether including these significant effects of MMT and treatment type on the right frontoparietal network played a role with regard to the cocaine-related effects seen in this network, including baseline years of cocaine use and negative toxicologies. When including these effects of MMT or treatment received in subsequent repeated-measures models of right-frontoparietal engagement with covariates of years of use and negative toxicology, previously-observed associations with lifetime years of use and negative toxicology were no longer significant (p-values>0.4). Individuals not completing treatment displayed greater reductions in average default-mode engagement at post-treatment (F 1,42 =5.89, p=0.020). Discussion Changes in the engagement of functional brain networks related to cognitive control were investigated in a longitudinal analysis of treatment-seeking individuals with CUD. ICA of Stroop fMRI identified five interference-related networks that were similar to those found previously including amygdala-striatal, medial-prefrontal, middle-frontal, and right- and left-frontoparietal networks ( Worhunsky et al., 2013 ). In addition, the default-mode network displayed longitudinal changes in engagement during Stroop performance. Contrary to hypotheses, interference-related networks were not differently engaged at post-treatment relative to baseline. However, when examining the influences of cocaine-use behaviors, including lifetime years of use and cocaine abstinence (percent of urines that were negative for cocaine) between scans, multiple networks displayed reduced engagement patterns over time, including amygdala-striatal, medial-prefrontal, and right-frontoparietal networks. The amygdala-striatal network displayed a reduction in interference engagement (i.e., incongruent vs congruent) at post-treatment relative to baseline that was limited in individuals with a longer history of cocaine use. Similarly, the medial-prefrontal network also showed reduced interference engagement over time when adjusting for lifetime duration cocaine use. The right-frontoparietal network displayed reduced interference engagement at both timepoints in individuals with longer use histories; however, greater abstinence between scans increased overall engagement at post-treatment relative to baseline, and network engagement was also influenced by treatment conditions. Primary findings suggest that functional networks in CUD may change over time, but that these changes may be differentially affected by use history, abstinence, and treatment administered. Taken together, the findings suggest that a longer history of cocaine use prior to entering treatment may impede changes in control-related neurocircuitry engagement, thus potentially hindering neurofunctional changes that promote recovery. Change in network engagement and baseline cocaine use Baseline use of cocaine appeared to contribute to observed engagement of the identified networks. Specifically, engagement of the amygdala-striatal network at baseline, consistent with a subcortical network implicated in reward and motivational processing ( Laird et al., 2011a ), was associated with years of use. When years of use was modeled, we observed that interference engagement in the amygdala-striatal network was reduced at the post-treatment scan. In our prior work, greater interference engagement of this functional network at baseline was linked prospectively to greater abstinence during treatment for CUD ( Worhunsky et al., 2013 ). Taken together, these data suggest that greater interference engagement of the amygdala-striatal network at baseline may indicate a readiness or capacity for change, while a reduction in use over time may maintain (or even increase) average functioning of the network during cognitive control. However, lifetime cocaine use appears to influence these dynamics. Exploratory correlations revealed that more years of use was associated with reduced interference engagement at baseline. Further, longer use histories limited the change in interference engagement over time. While not significant, the trending association between years of use and change in engagement of the medial-prefrontal network also suggest that potential change in cocaine-use behaviors, at least within the domain of cognitive control, may be somewhat reliant on duration of cocaine-use. Our findings suggest that increased lifetime duration of use may limit changes in engagement of this network, at least over the time period observed in these data. Associations between disease chronicity and functional and molecular impairments in mesocorticolimbic circuitry have been shown previously ( Gu et al., 2010 ; Worhunsky et al., 2017 ). To our knowledge, this is the first longitudinal evidence linking reduced functional change of an amygdala-striatal network, and to lesser extent a medial-prefrontal network, to the duration of CUD. These findings suggest that longer durations of cocaine use may contribute to inflexibility of subcortical circuitry and provide potential mechanistic support for why greater cocaine use severity predicts poorer treatment outcomes ( Ahmadi et al., 2006 ; Poling et al., 2007 ). Consistent with our prior investigations of these CUD cohorts separately using standard voxel-wise linear models ( DeVito et al., 2017 ; DeVito et al., 2019 ; DeVito et al., 2012 ), the current ICA analysis also indicated reductions in cognitive-control-related functional network engagement at post-treatment relative to baseline. Specifically a medial-prefrontal network, consistent with a prefrontal functional system associated with executive integration of cognitive and response processing ( Margulies et al., 2007 ; Zarr and Brown, 2016 ), was implicated. A right frontoparietal network, involved in inhibitory control and attention ( Roberts and Hall, 2008 ; Wang et al., 2018 ), demonstrated a relationship with years of use where cocaine-use history was associated with lower interference engagement at both time points. This finding suggests that longer durations of use may be associated with blunted executive function, in this case circuitry related to inhibiting and controlling attention. This could have ramifications for the tendency or ability to move attention away from drug-related cues. Change in network engagement and cocaine-use outcomes Greater average engagement of the amygdala-striatal and right-frontoparietal networks were associated with cocaine-abstinence measures during the treatment period. Engagement of the amygdala-striatal network was associated with a higher percentage of cocaine-negative urines, consistent with previous findings demonstrating that activation in subcortical networks predicts abstinence in people with CUD ( Yip et al., 2019 ). This finding and the finding that baseline years of use were associated with engagement indicate that substance-use patterns relate importantly to engagement of this reward-related network. The finding that percentage of cocaine-negative urines was associated with average engagement implies that this network is sensitive to short-term ramifications of substance use. Given findings that suggest this network is adaptable ( Yager et al., 2015 ) and theories that changes in engagement of this network accompany addictive behaviors ( Everitt and Robbins, 2013 ), our findings are not surprising. The right-frontoparietal network is commonly identified during Stroop performance ( McKeown and Sejnowski, 1998 ) and showed the highest amount of task-engagement compared to other networks. The right-frontoparietal network is involved in voluntary attentional control and task-switching ( MacDonald et al., 2000 ).Greater average engagement in this network as a function of cocaine use as measured by cocaine-negative urines and maximum days abstinent suggests that cocaine-use patterns may influence ability to flexibly redirect attention, perhaps away from drug-related cues( Kilts et al., 2014 ), although it should be noted this was a color-word Stroop and not a drug Stroop. However, no effects relating cocaine-use outcomes to change in engagement of this network were observed. Changes in engagement over time were also seen in the default-mode and middle-frontal networks when percentage of cocaine-negative urines was modeled. Observed changes related to abstinence in engagement of the default-mode network when cocaine-negative urines were taken into account resonate with previous findings related to the default-mode network and cocaine addiction ( Liang et al., 2015 ). Engagement of this network has been shown to statistically predict treatment outcomes ( Geng et al., 2017 ). Reductions in engagement of the middle-frontal network over time were also observed when controlling for cocaine-negative urines. This finding is consistent with previously observed inhibitory-control-related activations in middle-frontal areas including the middle frontal gyri and dorsolateral prefrontal cortices ( Kelly et al., 2004 ; McNab et al., 2008 ). These findings indicate that changes may occur in these networks, particularly when considering cocaine-use patterns. Further understanding of inhibitory-control tendencies and their correlates may help identify individuals requiring additional interventions ( Worhunsky et al., 2013 ) and shed light on what changes may occur in these networks over time, independent of drug-related factors. The trend-level correlation between change in engagement and cocaine-negative urines in the middle-frontal network implied that reduced change in this network over time was associated with sustained abstinence. This may imply that individuals who achieved abstinence early in treatment (100% cocaine-negative urines) showed less change over time in this network than individuals who had more variable use patterns, and that middle-frontal activation may closely relate to actual use behaviors. Given the trend-level correlation, however, future work in larger samples is needed. Overall, findings extend our previous findings using connectome-based modeling. Using data from a reward/loss processing fMRI task, within-network coordination of RFP and medial-prefrontal networks at treatment onset predicted within-treatment cocaine abstinence ( Yip et al., 2019 ). Using Stroop data, a separate set of networks involving between-network segregation of motor/sensory and both frontoparietal and medial-prefrontal networks predicted opioid abstinence ( Lichenstein et al., 2019 ). The cocaine and opioid abstinence networks were largely distinct and in each case generalized across other tasks ( Lichenstein et al., 2019 ). However, network strengths did not change significantly with treatment ( Lichenstein et al., 2019 ; Yip et al., 2019 ) in the prior analyses. Additional factors of longitudinal change in network engagement As our sample involved a treatment-seeking sample of individuals with CUD who underwent different treatment regimens, analyses were employed to determine if treatment factors influenced network engagement. Treatment factors of interest included concurrent MMT, the type of treatment received and whether treatment was completed. Our findings revealed relationships with treatment-related factors, specifically MMT and treatment received, on change over time in the engagement of the right-frontoparietal network. The effect of MMT on change over time in this network, however, suggests a potential mechanism for how methadone, or perhaps underlying opioid-use disorder, may impact neural correlates of executive performance and changes over time in treatment ( Wang et al., 2015 ), consistent with findings that have identified inhibitory control as an important predictor of treatment outcome ( Bell et al., 2014 ; Morie et al., 2014 ; Vergara-Moragues et al., 2017 ). Change over time in the engagement of the default-mode network was related to whether or not individuals successfully completed treatment. Individuals who did not complete tended to show greater suppression of this network overall. This may reflect increased effort to perform the task, while those who did successfully complete treatment may find the task less effortful, reflected in more engagement of the default-mode network. This result resonates with findings ( Worhunsky et al., 2021 ) that individuals with lower binding of D2 receptors during PET showed greater default-mode network suppression, potentially indicative that those individuals found the Stroop task to be more demanding. However, direct examination of this possibility is warranted. Finally, there was no indication that treatment-related factors, with the exception of MMT, influenced the relationships between cocaine outcome factors and changes in engagement of the right-frontoparietal or default-mode networks. The findings suggest that while changes over time exist in engagement of multiple networks, these changes may come about in association with use patterns rather than explicitly treatment-related factors Summary: Clinical implications Findings suggest that cortical networks related to cognitive control are variable, as we noted changes over time when cocaine-use patterns were modeled. In addition, our findings extend prior findings ( DeVito et al., 2017 ; DeVito et al., 2019 ; DeVito et al., 2012 ), which suggested that activation related to cognitive control is reduced over time. We similarly found reduction in interference engagement in the right frontoparietal network and amygdala-striatal network over time. However, our data identified that baseline variables, including years of cocaine use, may account for or limit the amount of change in these networks. Further, our findings indicated that greater interference engagement of a right-frontoparietal network, on average across both baseline and post-treatment scans, was associated with more abstinence (cocaine-negative urines) during treatment. This resonates with prior findings ( Bell et al., 2014 ; Connolly et al., 2012b ; Morie et al., 2014 ), indicating that greater than normal inhibitory control may be necessary to maintain abstinence. Taken together, findings suggest that when focusing on neural correlates of cognitive-control processes in treatment, it is important to consider individual differences in network engagement and potentially reduced cognitive flexibility in reward-related networks (such as the amygdala-striatal network) that relates to longer durations of cocaine use. Findings suggest that it may be important to consider duration of use when designing treatment plans for CUD and understanding how treatment works from a brain mechanistic perspective. Speculatively, individuals with longer use histories may require alternative or augmented treatments plans to address neural inflexibility. Clinicians may need to focus more on executive function training in individuals with long use histories in order to overcome inflexible and persistent reward-driven neurobiology, and this possibility warrants further, direct examination. Strengths and limitations Strengths of this study include a relatively large sample size (n=44) for longitudinal clinical imaging of CUD. Weaknesses include diverse treatment regimens, with participants drawn from three different treatment studies. Further, exploratory analyses collapsed treatments into three types (CBT, CBT+ and TAU), increasing heterogeneity in each group, and may have further limited the detection of specific treatment mechanisms on network activity. However, this can also be considered a strength as it allowed for conclusions to be drawn about changes in engagement of functional networks related to changes in cocaine use while considering a range of treatments. While the focus of the study was on functional networks, lack of in-scanner behavioral data limited analysis of performance factors. In addition, participants were categorized as treatment “completers” on the basis of whether or not they dropped out of treatment. It should be acknowledged that this in an imperfect measure of actual treatment dose received, as participants may have attended few treatment sessions despite staying in contact throughout the study. Similarly, the nature of this study was not to characterize the brain circuitry related to successful CUD recovery (no longer meeting criteria for CUD), but to investigate the longitudinal dynamics of functional networks related to changes in cocaine-use behavior. A limitation of longitudinal CUD research is the confounding bias of individuals who complete longitudinal research procedures. Individuals who do not complete treatment may present with different results. Further, use of urine and self-report abstinence measures, while indicated to be reliable measures of treatment success ( Carroll et al., 2014 ), may not fully capture the breadth of drug use changes that occur during treatment. Alternate measures of improvement may include percent reduction in use or achievement of 3 weeks of consecutive abstinence, and future work in larger samples may explore these measures. Additionally, we reported on trend-level correlations; these analyses were exploratory and require testing in larger, more homogenous samples. Finally, this study lacks a non-using control group, and thus comparisons to healthy test-retest changes in Stroop-related network engagement and exclusion of potential changes related to time, substance use or other factors was not possible. These should be examined in future research. Conclusions In sum, the current findings related to cocaine-use outcomes extend prior network-based models by demonstrating that changes in engagement of specific networks may occur over time in individuals with CUD, and that aspects of cocaine-use patterns may influence changes in engagement. Importantly, a longer history of cocaine use prior to treatment may impede changes in engagement of control-related circuitry in a manner that may impact recovery processes. Thus, future studies should consider effects of pre-treatment cocaine use in studies of mechanisms underlying treatment outcomes in CUD. Future work should also examine how specific treatments may impact engagement of these networks in larger samples and over longer durations, and how network engagement changes in abstinent individuals with CUD compare to those of healthy subjects without CUD. Supplementary Material 1 NIHMS1754754-supplement-1.docx (37.3KB, docx) Highlights. Longitudinal alterations in functional neural networks associated with cognitive control in cocaine use disorder (CUD) were examined using independent component analysis (ICA). Engagement of amygdala-striatal, middle frontal and right-fronto-parietal networks were reduced between baseline and post-treatment visits. Less change in the amygdala-striatal network was associated with greater lifetime years of cocaine use. Cocaine-negative toxicology and continuous abstinence were associated with engagement of the right-fronto parietal network Findings provide insight into how neural processes that underlie cognitive control may change over time and with respect to changes in cocaine use behaviors. Funding and disclosures Funding for this work included the National Institute on Drug Abuse (NIDA) grants K01 DA042937, K01 DA042998, and R01 DA039136. Original data collection was supported in part by K05 DA00457, K05 DA00089, P50 DA09241, R37 DA015969, R01 DA019078, R01 DA020908, and R01 DA035058. The funding agencies had no further role in study design; in the collection, analysis and interpretation of data; in the writing of the report; or in the decision to submit the paper for publication. Footnotes Conflict of Interest The authors report that they have no financial conflicts of interest with respect to the content of this manuscript. Dr. Potenza has received financial support or compensation for the following: Dr. Potenza has consulted for and advised RiverMend Health, Opiant Pharmaceuticals, Idorsia, the Addiction Policy Forum and AXA; has received research support from the Mohegan Sun Casino and the National Center for Responsible Gaming; has participated in surveys, mailings or telephone consultations related to addictive disorders or other health topics; has consulted for or advised law offices and gambling entities on issues related to addictive disorders and behaviors; has provided clinical care in the Connecticut Department of Mental Health and Addiction Services Problem Gambling Services Program; has performed grant reviews; has edited journals and journal sections; has given academic lectures in grand rounds, CME events and other clinical or scientific venues; and has generated books or book chapters for publishers of mental health texts. Dr. Potenza has participated in World Health Organization meetings relating to considerations on gaming and gambling. Dr. Potenza has participated in research meetings and groups focused on problematic internet use including with respect to gaming with support from European and Asian funding agencies. Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. References Abou-Elseoud A, Starck T, Remes J, Nikkinen J, Tervonen O, Kiviniemi V, 2010. The effect of model order selection in group PICA. Human brain mapping 31, 1207–1216. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Ahmadi J, Kampman K, Dackis C, 2006. Outcome predictors in cocaine dependence treatment trials. Am J Addict 15, 434–439. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Allen EA, Erhardt EB, Damaraju E, Gruner W, Segall JM, Silva RF, Havlicek M, Rachakonda S, Fries J, Kalyanam R, Michael AM, Caprihan A, Turner JA, Eichele T, Adelsheim S, Bryan AD, Bustillo J, Clark VP, Feldstein Ewing SW, Filbey F, Ford CC, Hutchison K, Jung RE, Kiehl KA, Kodituwakku P, Komesu YM, Mayer AR, Pearlson GD, Phillips JP, Sadek JR, Stevens M, Teuscher U, Thoma RJ, Calhoun VD, 2011. A baseline for the multivariate comparison of resting-state networks. Frontiers in systems neuroscience 5, 2. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Bell RP, Foxe JJ, Ross LA, Garavan H, 2014. Intact inhibitory control processes in abstinent drug abusers (I): a functional neuroimaging study in former cocaine addicts. Neuropharmacology 82, 143–150. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Calhoun