 M, Germiller JA, et al. Venous malformations of the temporal bone are a common feature in CHARGE syndrome. Laryngoscope 2012;122:895–900 10.1002/lary.23205 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Mahdi E, Whitehead MT. Clival malformations in CHARGE syndrome. AJNR Am J Neuroradiol 2018;39:1153–56 10.3174/ajnr.A5612 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Issekutz KA, Graham JM, Prasad C, et al. An epidemiological analysis of CHARGE syndrome: preliminary results from a Canadian study. Am J Med Genet A 2005;133:309–17 10.1002/ajmg.a.30560 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Dashti SR, Spetzler RF, Park MS, et al. Multimodality treatment of a complex cervicocerebral arteriovenous shunt in a patient with CHARGE syndrome: case report. Neurosurgery 2010;67:E208–09 10.1227/01.NEU.0000371064.91216.AB [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Bech AP, Op Den Akker J, Matthijsse PR. Isolation of the left subclavian artery from the pulmonary artery in a patient with CHARGE association. Congenit Anom (Kyoto). 2010;50:200–02 10.1111/j.1741-4520.2010.00283.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Ghalili K, Issenberg HJ, Freeman NJ, et al. Isolated left carotid artery in CHARGE association: diagnosis and repair. Ann Thorac Surg 1990;50:130–32 10.1016/0003-4975(90)90105-F [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Azab W, Delashaw J, Mohammed M. Persistent primitive trigeminal artery: a review. Turk Neurosurg 2011;22:399–406 10.5137/1019-5149.JTN.4427-11.1 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Meckel S, Spittau B, McAuliffe W. The persistent trigeminal artery: development, imaging anatomy, variants, and associated vascular pathologies. Neuroradiology 2013;55:5–16 10.1007/s00234-011-0995-3 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Saltzman GF. Patent primitive trigeminal artery studied by cerebral angiography. Acta Radiol 1959;51:329–36 10.3109/00016925909171103 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. McKenzie JD, Dean BL, Flom RA. Trigeminal-cavernous fistula: Saltzman anatomy revisited. AJNR Am J Neuroradiol 1996;17:280–82 [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Diana F, Mangiafico S, Valente V, et al. Persistent trigeminal artery aneurysms: case report and systematic review. J Neurointerv Surg 2019;11:1261–65 10.1136/neurintsurg-2019-015046 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. van Ravenswaaij-Arts CM, Hefner M, Blake K, et al. CHD7 disorder. GeneReviews October 2, 2006. Updated September 17, 2020. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK1117/ . Accessed May 4, 2021 26. Zentner GE, Layman WS, Martin DM, et al. Molecular and phenotypic aspects of CHD7 mutation in CHARGE syndrome. Am J Med Genet A 2010;152:674–86 10.1002/ajmg.a.33323 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Fields WS. The significance of persistent trigeminal artery: carotid-basilar anastomosis. Radiology 1968;91:1096–1101 10.1148/91.6.1096 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Menshawi K, Mohr JP, Gutierrez J. A functional perspective on the embryology and anatomy of the cerebral blood supply. J Stroke 2015;17:144–58 10.5853/jos.2015.17.2.144 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. de Geus CM, Bergman JE, van Ravenswaaij-Arts CM, et al. Imaging of clival hypoplasia in CHARGE syndrome and hypothesis for development: a case-control study. AJNR Am J Neuroradiol 2018;39:1938–42 10.3174/ajnr.A5810 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Wright JN, Rutledge J, Doherty D, et al. Cerebellar heterotopias: expanding the phenotype of cerebellar dysgenesis in CHARGE syndrome. AJNR Am J Neuroradiol 2019;40:2154–60 10.3174/ajnr.A6280 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',71,'2025-10-15 09:55:49','2025-10-15 09:55:49');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4460,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12069635','Impact of pharmacology perception and learning strategies on academic achievement in undergraduate pharmacy students.','Mengesha AK, Beyna AT, Chanie GS, Taye WA, Alemayehu TT, Bizuneh GK, Aragie YS, Geremew GW, Ayele HS.','2025','gondar rote overemphasis gondar deepens pharmacotherapeutics spq underexplored rote detracts rote gondar gondar guilding caramona pedagogies igi mclain quesnelle icap e00762 fasinu wilborn e1178 khurshid carvalhais aacp alsanosi meenu sibbald ghanbari systematized curric dzaiy zanco hilsmann mcmullen kalungia metacognitive zagaar boedeker pharmacore barash mckeirnan ghazi tabatabaee afrashteh siami bressane chaleta saraiva jarni gurr khurshid vermunt volkov cult murtonen urgo arguello shehzadi afridi farwa fokkens bruinsma kavyar torbatinezgad ghandizadeh pouyesh kulo cestone taconis bekker caskey e0246676 rockinson szapkiw khine idrizi filiposka trajkovikj multimed kerins rarieya pedagogies gazar khurshid rata mohamad nasri mahmud','Abstract Pharmacology is a cornerstone of pharmacy education, bridging biomedical sciences with clinical application. Understanding students’ perceptions of pharmacology’s relevance can influence their learning strategies and academic performance. Despite its significance, gaps remain in understanding how different learning strategies impact pharmacology education. This study aimed to assess the perceived role of pharmacology education, the learning strategies adopted by undergraduate pharmacy students, and their impact on academic achievement across different academic years. Additionally, internal consistency and factor load analysis of the applied questionnaire were evaluated to ensure reliability. A cross-sectional study was conducted at the University of Gondar from June 1, 2024, to August 30, 2024, involving 210 undergraduate pharmacy students from second to fifth year. Data were collected using a structured, self-administered questionnaire that assessed demographics, perceptions of pharmacology, learning strategies, and academic achievement. Descriptive and inferential statistics, including Pearson’s correlation, multiple regression analysis, internal consistency (Cronbach’s alpha), and factor load analysis, were performed using SPSS version 26. The study included 120 male (57.1%) and 90 female participants (42.9%). Participants reported engaging in deep learning strategies (mean score: 3.75) more frequently than surface learning strategies (mean score: 2.85). A strong positive correlation ( r = 0.72, p < 0.01) was found between academic achievement and deep learning strategies, while surface learning showed a negative correlation ( r = −0.34, p < 0.01). One-way ANOVA revealed significant differences in deep learning strategy use across academic years (F = 5.23, p = 0.002). Factor load analysis confirmed that the questionnaire effectively captured constructs related to pharmacology perceptions and learning strategies, with a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.85, indicating good internal consistency. The findings highlight the importance of pharmacology in pharmacy education and its impact on academic success. Encouraging deep learning strategies and enhancing students’ perceptions of pharmacology may improve educational outcomes. Future research should incorporate longitudinal studies to track changes in learning strategies over time and assess the long-term impact of pharmacology education. Keywords: Pharmacology, Learning strategies, Academic achievement, Pharmacy education, Undergraduate students Subject terms: Cardiology, Endocrinology, Medical research Introduction Pharmacology is a cornerstone of pharmacy education, critical for bridging the biomedical sciences with clinical application 1 , 2 . Understanding how students perceive pharmacology’s relevance to their careers and its practical utility has been shown to influence their choice of learning strategies, potentially impacting their academic performance and professional readiness 3 , 4 . Research highlights that students who view pharmacology as clinically significant are more likely to engage in deep learning approaches, emphasizing understanding and application over rote memorization 5 , 6 . This strategy is linked to better retention and outcomes, which are essential as pharmacology forms the basis for therapeutic knowledge required for patient care and licensure exams 7 . Moreover, pharmacology education plays a crucial role in preparing students for evidence-based clinical decision-making and the safe prescribing of medications, which are essential competencies for pharmacists and other healthcare professionals 8 . A well-structured pharmacology curriculum contributes to improved patient care by equipping students with the ability to critically evaluate drug efficacy, safety, and interactions in diverse clinical scenarios 9 . However, despite its importance, pharmacology education faces challenges in engagement and effectiveness, often due to passive learning approaches and an overemphasis on memorization rather than conceptual understanding 5 . In contrast, students who rely primarily on surface learning methods, such as memorization, often face challenges in retaining complex pharmacological concepts, which may hinder their long-term performance and comprehension of therapeutic principles 10 , 11 . Curricular strategies that integrate pharmacology with related courses and employ interactive, case-based learning tools have been suggested as effective ways to enhance student engagement and depth of understanding 5 , 12 . Recent studies emphasize the importance of adopting active learning methodologies, such as problem-based learning (PBL), flipped classrooms, and simulation-based education, to enhance students’ ability to apply pharmacological knowledge in clinical practice 13 . Research Gap: Despite extensive research on learning strategies in medical and pharmacy education, there is limited evidence on how different learning strategies influence academic success in pharmacology across various academic levels. Most existing studies focus on general learning approaches without distinguishing the unique impact of pharmacology as a subject that integrates foundational science with clinical application 14 . Furthermore, few studies have evaluated the effectiveness of pharmacology teaching methods through validated psychometric tools, highlighting the need for robust assessments to measure students’ perceptions and learning strategies 15 . Addressing these gaps is crucial for improving pharmacology curricula and optimizing educational outcomes. Given these dynamics, analyzing how pharmacy students across different academic years perceive pharmacology and approach learning can provide insights for refining educational methods to better support students’ academic success and clinical competency 16 , 17 . This study aims to assess the perceived role of pharmacology education, the learning strategies adopted by undergraduate pharmacy students, and the impact of these strategies on academic achievement across different academic years. By investigating these factors, the study seeks to identify key elements that influence students’ understanding of pharmacology and contribute to their overall academic success. In doing so, it aims to provide valuable insights for optimizing teaching methods and supporting student outcomes. Additionally, this study explores how instructional design, assessment methods, and faculty engagement contribute to students’ learning experiences in pharmacology education 18 . Hypotheses: H1: Students’ perceptions of the role of pharmacology are significantly associated with their academic achievement, and this relationship varies across different academic years. H2: The learning strategies adopted by pharmacy students have a significant impact on their academic achievement in pharmacology courses. H3: Students’ perceptions of pharmacology’s role and their preferred learning strategies differ significantly across academic years, reflecting evolving needs and preferences as they progress through the program (Fig. 1 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Conceptual framework illustrating the relationship between perception of pharmacology, learning strategies, and academic achievement. Methods Study design and setting This cross-sectional study was conducted to assess the perceived role of pharmacology, learning strategies, and academic achievement among undergraduate pharmacy students. The study took place at the University of Gondar from June 1, 2024 to August 30, 2024. Population and sampling The study targeted the entire population of 210 pharmacy students in their second, third, fourth, and fifth years of study. The focus of the research was on the general understanding of pharmacology, rather than a specific pharmacology course. This distinction is important because pharmacology education in the pharmacy curriculum typically spans multiple years, with students gradually building on foundational concepts in later years. The study aimed to assess how students’ learning strategies and perceived roles of pharmacology education across these years influence their academic achievement. Participants were recruited through classroom announcements and official university emails sent to all eligible students. A census approach was adopted since the entire eligible population ( n = 210) was targeted, ensuring maximum representativeness and transparency in sample selection. The curriculum in this particular pharmacy program follows a scaffolding approach, where the study of pharmacology begins in the early years with a focus on foundational concepts and progressively deepens in complexity as students move through the program. In the first few years, students are introduced to basic pharmacology, which includes understanding drug mechanisms and their effects on the body. In the later years, more advanced topics are covered, such as pharmacotherapeutics, drug interactions, and clinical applications of pharmacology, often in conjunction with other subjects like medicinal chemistry and therapeutics. Given this progression, senior students (in their fourth and fifth years) are exposed to a much more advanced level of pharmacology compared to junior students (in their second and third years). This difference in exposure raises an important consideration for the study—whether these two cohorts are comparable in terms of their level of understanding and academic achievement in pharmacology. It is possible that senior students, who have completed more advanced pharmacology coursework and have a deeper understanding, may have different learning strategies and academic outcomes compared to junior students. To address this, the study carefully considered the influence of year level on students’ learning strategies, ensuring that any analysis accounted for the progressive nature of pharmacology education. Additionally, pharmacology in this program is integrated with other key subjects, such as medicinal chemistry and pharmacokinetics, providing students with a holistic understanding of drug action and its applications. This integration supports the development of critical thinking and the ability to apply pharmacological knowledge in clinical settings. The study sought to examine how these integrated learning experiences, in combination with the student’s learning strategies, influenced their overall academic success in pharmacology. By targeting all students in the second through fifth years, the study used a census approach to enhance the representativeness of the sample. This method allowed for a broad understanding of how pharmacology education impacts students across different stages of their academic journey. It also provided insights into the different learning strategies employed by students at various levels of exposure to the subject, offering a comprehensive view of how pharmacology education contributes to their academic achievement. Inclusion criteria Enrollment in the undergraduate pharmacy program in the specified years (second through fifth year) and completion of at least one pharmacology course. Exclusion criteria Students who were in their first year of study, or who had not yet completed any pharmacology courses, were excluded from the study. This exclusion criterion was established because first-year students typically have not yet encountered pharmacology content in their curriculum, making their inclusion in the study irrelevant to the research objectives. Additionally, students who were not present during data collection or who did not provide consent to participate were also excluded. These exclusions helped ensure that the sample consisted only of students with sufficient exposure to pharmacology coursework, allowing for a more accurate assessment of the factors influencing their understanding and academic achievement in the subject. Data collection tools and procedures Data were collected using a structured, self-administered questionnaire, which was adapted and modified from previously published questionnaires to gather comprehensive information across multiple areas. First, demographics were assessed, including items on age, gender, and academic year, which helped contextualize participants’ backgrounds and control for potential confounding variables in the analysis. Next, perceptions of pharmacology were measured using Likert-scale items (1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree), focusing on students’ views on the relevance of pharmacology to clinical practice, its impact on their careers, and their engagement with pharmacology coursework 19 . The questionnaire was distributed during scheduled class sessions to ensure high participation rates and consistency in the data collection process. Trained research assistants provided standardized instructions and were available to clarify any questions, thereby enhancing the reliability and reproducibility of the data collection. The questionnaire also included a section on learning strategies, adapted from the Study Process Questionnaire (SPQ), to evaluate students’ approaches to learning. This section covered both deep learning strategies (e.g., understanding and applying pharmacological concepts) and surface learning strategies (e.g., memorization for exams), with students rating the frequency and importance of each approach in their pharmacology studies 20 , 21 . Lastly, academic achievement was measured through self-reported GPA and grades specifically in pharmacology courses, facilitating an analysis of the relationship between students’ perceptions, learning strategies, and academic outcomes. The questionnaire was pilot-tested with a small subset of students ( n = 20), who were not included in the main sample, to assess its clarity, reliability, and validity. This pilot test allowed for the refinement of the instrument before the full-scale study. Additionally, internal consistency was evaluated using Cronbach’s alpha, yielding a coefficient of 0.85, indicating good internal consistency across different sections of the questionnaire. Factor load analysis was conducted to assess the construct validity of the questionnaire, with key dimensions showing factor loadings ranging from 0.60 to 0.82, aligning well with theoretical frameworks and ensuring the robustness of the measurement model. Data analysis Data were analyzed using Statistical Package for the Social Sciences (SPSS) version 26. The analysis began with descriptive statistics, including means, standard deviations, frequencies, and percentages, to provide an overview of the demographics and academic characteristics of the participants. For inferential statistics, Pearson’s correlation was used to examine relationships between students’ perceptions of pharmacology, their learning strategies, and academic achievement. Additionally, a one-way ANOVA was conducted to explore any differences in perceptions and learning strategies across different academic years. Finally, a multiple regression analysis was performed to identify significant predictors of academic achievement, controlling for demographic variables and academic year, to provide deeper insights into factors influencing student performance. Reliability analysis (Cronbach’s alpha) was also conducted to validate the measurement tools. Results Demographic profile of study participants Among the participants, the gender distribution shows that 120 individuals, or 57.1%, were male, while 90 participants, representing 42.9%, were female. The age group breakdown reveals that 50 participants (23.8%) were aged between 18 and 20 years. The majority, 90 participants, or 42.9%, fell within the 21 to 23-year age range. Additionally, 70 participants, making up 33.3% of the total, were 24 years of age or older. Regarding the academic year, 50 participants (23.8%) were in their second year of study, and 60 participants, or 28.6%, were in their third year. Fourth-year students accounted for 55 participants (26.2%), while those in their fifth year comprised 45 participants, representing 21.4% of the study population (Table 1 ). Table 1. Demographic characteristics of participants. Characteristic n (%) Gender Male 120 (57.1) Female 90 (42.9) Age group 18–20 years 50 (23.8) 21–23 years 90 (42.9) 24 years and above 70 (33.3) Academic year Second Year 50 (23.8) Third Year 60 (28.6) Fourth Year 55 (26.2) Fifth Year 45 (21.4) Open in a new tab Descriptive statistics of deep and surface learning strategies among participants The analysis of learning strategies among participants, presented in Table 2 , highlights descriptive statistics for both deep and surface learning approaches. The mean score for deep learning strategies was 3.75 with a standard deviation (SD) of 0.45, and the scores ranged from 2.0 to 4.8. In contrast, surface learning strategies had a lower mean score of 2.85 with an SD of 0.60, with a range from 1.0 to 4.0 (Table 2 ). Table 2. Descriptive statistics for learning strategies. Learning strategy Mean ± SD Range (Min–Max) Deep learning strategies 3.75 ± 0.45 2.0–4.8 Surface learning strategies 2.85 ± 0.60 1.0–4.0 Open in a new tab Correlation analysis of academic achievement, perception of pharmacology, and learning strategies Table 3 provides a Pearson correlation analysis, showing the relationships between academic achievement, perception of pharmacology, and both deep and surface learning strategies. A strong positive correlation ( r = 0.72, p < 0.01) was observed between academic achievement and deep learning strategies. There was also a moderate positive correlation between academic achievement and perception of pharmacology ( r = 0.56, p < 0.01). Surface learning strategies, however, showed a negative correlation with academic achievement ( r = −0.34, p < 0.01). Table 3. Pearson correlation analysis. Variable Academic achievement Perception of pharmacology Deep learning strategies Surface learning strategies Academic achievement 1.00 0.56** 0.72** −0.34** Perception of pharmacology 0.56** 1.00 0.45** −0.25* Deep learning strategies 0.72** 0.45** 1.00 −0.15 Surface learning strategies −0.34** −0.25* −0.15 1.00 Open in a new tab *Note: ** p < 0.01; * p < 0.05. Additionally, deep learning strategies were positively correlated with the perception of pharmacology ( r = 0.45, p < 0.01). Surface learning strategies, on the other hand, showed a slight negative correlation with the perception of pharmacology ( r = −0.25, p < 0.05). There was also a small, non-significant negative correlation between deep and surface learning strategies ( r = −0.15) (Table 3 ). One-way ANOVA analysis of learning strategies across academic years The results of the one-way ANOVA analysis, presented in Table 4 , examine differences in learning strategies (deep and surface) across various academic years. The mean scores for deep learning strategies varied slightly among the academic years, with second-year students showing the highest mean score of 3.80 (SD = 0.40), followed by fourth-year students at 3.85 (SD = 0.50). Third-year students had a mean score of 3.70 (SD = 0.45), and fifth-year students scored the lowest on deep learning strategies with a mean of 3.60 (SD = 0.55). These differences yielded a statistically significant F-value of 5.23 with a p-value of 0.002. Table 4. One-way ANOVA results for learning strategies across academic years. Academic year Mean ± SD (Deep learning) Mean ± SD (Surface learning) F-value p -value Second year 3.80 ± 0.40 2.70 ± 0.50 5.23 0.002 Third year 3.70 ± 0.45 2.90 ± 0.55 Fourth year 3.85 ± 0.50 2.80 ± 0.60 Fifth year 3.60 ± 0.55 3.10 ± 0.65 Open in a new tab For surface learning strategies, the mean scores also showed variation across academic years. Second-year students had a mean score of 2.70 (SD = 0.50), and third-year students scored slightly higher at 2.90 (SD = 0.55). Fourth-year students had a mean of 2.80 (SD = 0.60), while fifth-year students demonstrated the highest mean score of 3.10 (SD = 0.65) in surface learning strategies (Table 4 ). Multiple regression analysis of predictors of academic achievement The results of the multiple regression analysis provide insights into various predictors of academic achievement among students. The predictors include age group, gender, perceptions of pharmacology, and academic year. The analysis revealed significant differences in academic achievement based on age. Students aged 21–23 years demonstrated a positive relationship with academic performance (B = 0.35, 95% CI = [0.11, 0.59], p = 0.004), as did those aged 24 years and above (B = 0.50, 95% CI = [0.15, 0.85], p = 0.007). In contrast, the 18–20 age group showed no significant impact (B = 0.20, 95% CI = [−0.10, 0.50], p = 0.18). Gender also emerged as a significant predictor. Male students had a notable positive effect on academic achievement (B = 0.40, 95% CI = [0.20, 0.60], p = 0.001), while female students showed a weaker and non-significant relationship (B = 0.20, 95% CI = [−0.05, 0.45], p = 0.10). Students’ perceptions regarding the relevance of pharmacology to clinical practice had a strong positive influence on their academic success. Specifically, the belief in its relevance (B = 0.45, 95% CI = [0.27, 0.63], p = 0.001), impact on career aspirations (B = 0.30, 95% CI = [0.10, 0.50], p = 0.003), engagement with coursework (B = 0.35, 95% CI = [0.13, 0.57], p = 0.002), self-efficacy (B = 0.40, 95% CI = [0.20, 0.60], p = 0.001), and understanding of pharmacology’s importance (B = 0.50, 95% CI = [0.32, 0.68], p = 0.001) were all significant predictors. Lastly, the academic year plays a crucial role in predicting students’ academic performance. As students progressed through their studies, those in the fourth year (B = 0.40, 95% CI = [0.16, 0.64], p = 0.001) and fifth year (B = 0.50, 95% CI = [0.29, 0.71], p = 0.001) showed significant positive associations with academic achievement. Meanwhile, second-year showed a weaker and non-significant relationship (B = 0.25, 95% CI = [−0.05, 0.55], p = 0.10) and third-year (B = 0.30, 95% CI = [0.05, 0.55], p = 0.02) students exhibited weaker but still notable relationships (Table 5 ). Table 5. Multiple regression analysis for predictors of academic achievement. Predictor B Standard error 95% confidence interval Beta p -values Age group 18–20 years 0.20 0.15 [−0.10, 0.50] 0.10 0.18 21–23 years 0.35 0.12 [0.11, 0.59] 0.25 0.004 24 years and above 0.50 0.18 [0.15, 0.85] 0.20 0.007 Gender Male 0.40 0.10 [0.20, 0.60] 0.30 0.001 Female 0.20 0.12 [−0.05, 0.45] 0.15 0.10 Perception of pharmacology Relevance to clinical practice 0.45 0.09 [0.27, 0.63] 0.40 0.001 Impact on career aspirations 0.30 0.10 [0.10, 0.50] 0.25 0.003 Engagement with coursework 0.35 0.11 [0.13, 0.57] 0.30 0.002 Self-efficacy and confidence 0.40 0.10 [0.20, 0.60] 0.35 0.001 Understanding of pharmacology’s importance 0.50 0.09 [0.32, 0.68] 0.45 0.001 Academic year Second year 0.25 0.15 [−0.05, 0.55] 0.20 0.10 Third year 0.30 0.13 [0.05, 0.55] 0.25 0.02 Fourth-year 0.40 0.12 [0.16, 0.64] 0.35 0.001 Fifth year 0.50 0.11 [0.29, 0.71] 0.40 0.001 Open in a new tab Significant values are in [bold]. Discussion This study aimed to investigate the relationship between learning strategies—specifically deep and surface learning—and academic achievement, while also examining the influence of demographic factors such as age, gender, and academic year. By analyzing these factors, the study provides insights into how pharmacology education impacts student success and offers valuable recommendations for optimizing teaching practices in the pharmacy curriculum. A critical evaluation of these findings against existing literature suggests that while deep learning is consistently linked to better academic outcomes, the effectiveness of various pedagogical interventions remains underexplored 22 . The analysis of learning strategies revealed significant variations among participants in their engagement with deep and surface learning. The mean score for deep learning strategies was 3.75 (SD = 0.45), suggesting that most students engaged with their learning in a way that promoted understanding and retention. This finding aligns with previous studies showing that deep learning approaches are linked to better comprehension, long-term retention, and overall academic success 23 , 24 . Deep learning strategies, such as critical thinking and self-directed learning, allow students to integrate new knowledge into existing frameworks, enhancing their ability to apply pharmacological concepts in clinical settings 25 . However, some studies suggest that deep learning alone may not be sufficient, and a combination of structured guidance and self-regulated learning could be more effective in improving retention and application of pharmacology concepts 26 . Conversely, surface learning strategies, which tend to focus on rote memorization and minimal engagement with the material, had a lower mean score of 2.85 (SD = 0.60). This indicates that surface learning strategies were less commonly employed, but still prevalent among some participants. Previous research has consistently found that surface learning is associated with lower academic achievement, as it often fails to promote a deep understanding of complex subjects like pharmacology 27 – 29 . Students relying more on surface learning may struggle to apply theoretical knowledge in practical contexts, an essential skill in the pharmaceutical field 30 . A review of prior studies highlights that surface learning may persist due to assessment methods that emphasize recall over application, suggesting the need for reform in evaluation techniques 31 . Pearson correlation analysis revealed a strong positive relationship between deep learning strategies and academic achievement ( r = 0.72, p < 0.01). This is consistent with the body of literature highlighting the importance of deep learning strategies in promoting higher academic performance 32 . Students who engage deeply with course material are better equipped to integrate and retain pharmacological knowledge, leading to improved exam scores and better clinical decision-making abilities. A moderate correlation ( r = 0.56, p < 0.01) was found between academic achievement and students’ perceptions of pharmacology. This suggests that students with positive perceptions of pharmacology—such as viewing it as relevant to clinical practice and integral to their future careers—are more likely to perform better academically. Similar studies have demonstrated that positive attitudes toward a subject enhance motivation, which in turn can lead to improved academic outcomes 33 , 34 . Encouraging students to see the value of pharmacology within the broader context of clinical practice could foster greater engagement and deeper learning. This suggests that curriculum adjustments should focus on increasing the perceived relevance of pharmacology through interdisciplinary teaching and practical case-based scenarios 35 . In contrast, surface learning strategies displayed a negative correlation with academic achievement ( r = −0.34, p < 0.01), reinforcing the idea that superficial engagement with pharmacological content detracts from meaningful learning. This finding aligns with earlier research, which suggests that surface approaches are linked to lower levels of understanding and poorer long-term retention 36 . Encouraging students to move beyond surface strategies and adopt more active and reflective learning techniques may help improve their overall academic performance. The analysis also examined the use of learning strategies across different academic years. It was found that second-year students had the highest mean score for deep learning strategies (3.80, SD = 0.40), while fifth-year students scored the lowest (3.60, SD = 0.55). This variation, although statistically significant (F = 5.23, p = 0.002), suggests that the use of deep learning strategies declines as students progress through their studies. This trend is not uncommon in educational research, where an increasing reliance on surface learning strategies is observed as students face more complex material and time constraints in later years 37 , 38 . These findings suggest that as students advance in their studies, they may become overwhelmed by the volume and complexity of content, leading to a shift toward more rote memorization and less critical engagement with the material. This highlights the need for structured faculty mentorship programs to support students in maintaining deep learning strategies throughout their academic journey 39 . Interestingly, the trend for surface learning strategies was the reverse, with fifth-year students showing the highest mean score (3.10, SD = 0.65) and second-year students the lowest (2.70, SD = 0.50). This trend suggests that older students may resort to surface learning strategies as they progress, possibly due to the cumulative and intensive nature of the curriculum. This shift in learning strategies over time may require educators to consider how to maintain student engagement and promote deep learning in later years of study when students are often under significant academic and personal pressures. Future research should investigate whether increased workload and exam stress contribute to this shift and whether curriculum modifications such as spaced learning can mitigate this effect 40 . The multiple regression analysis further identified several significant predictors of academic achievement, including age group, gender, perceptions of pharmacology, and academic year. Students aged 21–23 years (B = 0.35, 95% CI = [0.11, 0.59], p = 0.004) and those aged 24 years and above (B = 0.50, 95% CI = [0.15, 0.85], p = 0.007) demonstrated better academic performance, consistent with prior studies suggesting that older students tend to exhibit more maturity and motivation, which contribute to better academic outcomes 41 . In contrast, younger students (aged 18–20) did not show a significant impact, suggesting that early academic experiences may play a role in shaping academic performance later in the program. Gender differences were also noted, with male students showing a stronger positive relationship with academic achievement (B = 0.40, 95% CI = [0.20, 0.60], p = 0.001) compared to female students, whose relationship was weaker and non-significant. These findings align with studies indicating gender differences in academic performance, which may stem from varying learning styles, confidence levels, and external factors affecting academic achievement 42 – 44 . Recognizing these differences can help educators tailor support strategies to better address the needs of male and female students. This calls for gender-sensitive pedagogical strategies that accommodate diverse learning preferences and enhance equal academic opportunities 45 . Students’ perceptions of pharmacology were significant predictors of academic success. Those who believed in its relevance to clinical practice (B = 0.45, 95% CI = [0.27, 0.63], p = 0.001), perceived its impact on career aspirations (B = 0.30, 95% CI = [0.10, 0.50], p = 0.003), engaged with coursework (B = 0.35, 95% CI = [0.13, 0.57], p = 0.002), had high self-efficacy (B = 0.40, 95% CI = [0.20, 0.60], p = 0.001), and understood pharmacology’s importance (B = 0.50, 95% CI = [0.32, 0.68], p = 0.001) were more likely to achieve academically. These findings align with previous literature emphasizing the importance of student attitudes and beliefs in influencing academic performance 46 . Lastly, the academic year significantly impacted students’ academic performance. Fourth-year (B = 0.40, 95% CI = [0.16, 0.64], p = 0.001) and fifth-year (B = 0.50, 95% CI = [0.29, 0.71], p = 0.001) students demonstrated substantial positive associations with academic achievement. Meanwhile, third-year students had a moderate association (B = 0.30, 95% CI = [0.05, 0.55], p = 0.02). This trend reflects the cumulative nature of learning and the development of deeper understanding as students progress through their studies, which has been documented in prior educational research 47 . Moreover, these findings highlight the necessity for educators to actively cultivate positive perceptions of pharmacology through targeted teaching strategies, such as case-based learning and real-world clinical applications. By embedding pharmacology within practical, patient-centered scenarios, educators can strengthen students’ belief in its relevance and impact, potentially enhancing engagement and academic outcomes 48 . Lastly, the academic year significantly impacted students’ academic performance. Fourth-year (B = 0.40, 95% CI = [0.16, 0.64], p = 0.001) and fifth-year (B = 0.50, 95% CI = [0.29, 0.71], p = 0.001) students demonstrated substantial positive associations with academic achievement. Meanwhile, third-year students had a moderate association (B = 0.30, 95% CI = [0.05, 0.55], p = 0.02). This trend reflects the cumulative nature of learning and the development of deeper understanding as students progress through their studies, which has been documented in prior educational research 47 . These findings emphasize the importance of scaffolding pharmacology content across academic years. Educators should consider progressive curriculum models that introduce foundational concepts early and reinforce them with increasing complexity 49 . This structured approach can help sustain engagement and build confidence in pharmacological knowledge, ultimately leading to better academic outcomes. Additionally, integrating formative assessments and feedback mechanisms can further support students in recognizing their progress and addressing learning gaps. Previous studies have acknowledged the role of engagement and self-efficacy in academic success, there is a growing need to explore how instructional design influences these factors 50 . The findings from this study have critical implications for pharmacy educators and curriculum designers. The strong link between deep learning and academic achievement underscores the importance of developing teaching strategies that encourage active learning, self-reflection, and real-world application of knowledge 13 , 51 . Study limitations and recommendations The cross-sectional design of this study limits the ability to establish causal relationships between the perceived roles of pharmacology, learning strategies, and academic achievement. This design captures a snapshot of students’ perceptions and performance at a single point in time, making it challenging to draw definitive conclusions about cause and effect. Additionally, the sample size may not represent the entire population of pharmacy students, which could affect the generalizability of the findings to a broader context. Furthermore, the reliance on self-reported data introduces potential bias, as students may overestimate or underestimate their perceptions and academic performance. This subjectivity can affect the accuracy of the results. The study’s focus on a single institution also limits the applicability of the findings to other pharmacy programs that may have different curricula or student demographics. Lastly, the research did not account for external factors that may influence academic achievement, such as socioeconomic status, mental health, and personal circumstances, which could play a significant role in students’ success. To enhance future research, a longitudinal approach should be considered to better understand the causal relationships between perceptions of pharmacology, learning strategies, and academic success. By following the same cohort of students over time, researchers can gather more meaningful data on how these factors interact and evolve. Additionally, expanding the sample size to include multiple institutions can improve the generalizability of the results, allowing for a more comprehensive understanding of pharmacy education. Incorporating objective measures of academic achievement alongside self-reported data can also provide a more accurate assessment of student performance and help mitigate potential biases associated with self-reporting. Future research should explore the impact of external factors on academic achievement, considering variables such as mental health support and socioeconomic backgrounds, to create a more holistic understanding of the factors influencing student success. Moreover, future research should investigate the effectiveness of active learning methodologies, such as team-based learning and interactive simulations, in shaping students’ perceptions and academic performance in pharmacology. Faculty training programs should also emphasize instructional methods that move beyond traditional lecture-based approaches and integrate interactive, case-based, and experiential learning strategies. Conclusion This study underscores the importance of pharmacology in pharmacy education and highlights effective learning strategies as key determinants of academic success. Students who recognize the relevance of pharmacology and employ structured learning approaches tend to achieve better outcomes. To enhance pharmacology education, educators should implement active learning methods, integrate real-world applications, and provide continuous formative assessments. Addressing study limitations and refining pedagogical approaches can further improve learning experiences and academic performance among pharmacy students, ultimately strengthening their preparedness for professional practice. Acknowledgements The authors wish to express their sincere gratitude to all the undergraduate pharmacy students who participated in this study, as their insights and willingness to share their experiences made this research possible. We also extend our appreciation to the faculty and staff of the Department/School of Pharmacy, University of Gondar for their support and encouragement throughout the research process. Author contributions A.K.M., A.T.B., G.S.C., W.A.T., G.K.B., T.T.A., Y.S.A., and G.W.G. contributed to the drafting and revision of the manuscript. H.S.A. was responsible for preparing Tables 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 and 5 . All authors reviewed the manuscript. Funding The authors received no financial support for the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article. Data availability Data is provided within the manuscript. Declarations Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Ethical considerations and consent to participation Ethical clearance was obtained from the Institutional Review Board of the University of Gondar School of Pharmacy (SOP/73/2024). Written informed consent was obtained from all participants prior to data collection, and they were assured of the confidentiality and voluntary nature of their participation. Data were anonymized and securely stored to protect participant privacy. The study was conducted in compliance with the principles outlined in the Declaration of Helsinki 28 . Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Guilding, C. et al. Developing an international concept-based curriculum for Pharmacology education: the promise of core concepts and concept inventories. Br. J. Clin. Pharmacol. 9 (2), 1–16 (2023). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 2. de Almeida, M. J., Figueiredo, I. V. & Caramona, M. M. Perspectives on learning and teaching science: Influence on clinical pharmacy education. In Pedagogies for Pharmacy Curricula 1–19 (IGI Global, 2021). 3. Jane Collins, M. & McLain, N. E. Pharmacology course redesign using high-impact practices. J. Nurs. Educ. 60 (9), 529–533 (2021). [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Quesnelle, K. M. et al. Design of a foundational sciences curriculum: Applying the ICAP framework to pharmacology education in integrated medical curricula. Pharmacol. Res. Perspect. 9 (3), e00762 (2021). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Fasinu, P. S. & Wilborn, T. W. Pharmacology education in the medical curriculum: Challenges and opportunities for improvement. Pharmacol. Res. Perspect. 12 (1), e1178 (2024). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Khurshid, F. Using threshold concepts for the development of a guided approach to teaching, learning, and assessment of pharmacology (2023). 7. Sanders, K. M., Bloom, T. & Fonseca-Carvalhais, B. AACP REPORT. In Annual Meeting of the American Association of Colleges of Pharmacy (2022). 8. Alsanosi, S. M. A new vision of teaching clinical pharmacology and therapeutics for undergraduate medical students. Adv. Med. Educ. Pract. 13 , 567–575 (2022). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 9. Meenu, K. S., Yadav, S. K. & Yadav, D. Recent advances in pharmacogenomics and clin