enberg zenger brahler sss jbw atala garety delusional a007116 eterovic kozaric kovacic mehy nasreddine bedirian charbonneau chertkow tariq tumosa chibnall jgp holroyd wooten corlett krystal pneurobio seeman acebillo labad monreal delusional bs11100141 samudra womack khemani chitnis s40266 pappert stemer hinkle perepezko broen marvel pantelyat pletnikova pontone motoric mdc3 panisset suchowersky goodridge mendis ritunnano humpston broome meaningfulness bjpsych bjb fennig bromet landorf lavelle flaum schres corlett krystal garety persecutory s00127 garety hemsley wessely deluded schizophrenic paranoid nanagas mcna varsamis sigurdson schizophrenics hutchesson volans unsubstantiated mawson monreal natividad seeman delusional healthcare10091729 skelton khokhar thacker delusional cd009785 cd009785 monreal natividad seeman delusional biomedicines biomedicines10123281 seeman delusional ijerph17124583 seeman delusional wjp v12 zangrilli ducci bandinelli dooley priebe 244x metacognitive rudden frances fishburn a128 zilker xxii a055 clt redlich tonori aizama bf02908885 staudenmayer s96 rtph bornschein hausteiner zilker forstl s0033291702006554 levenson depping s0033291721004177 katon bailer witthoft bayerl rist somatoform jpsychores dunphy penna kafsi e231550 somatization bolla bleecker percept mot engell adkins 02110s4641 s0193 953x boeft claassen dessel wouden j268 j268 fischhoff wessely inexplicable henningsen dcns phenningsen kleinstauber witthoft steffanowski marwijk hiller somatoform cd010628 cd010628 koelen houtveen abbass somatoform schottenfeld brodsky somatoform schmaling asserting oldham psym okiishi schlosser perharic volans preservatives attributional birbaumer schottenfeld','Case Presentation A 71-year-old female with a history of Parkinson’s disease (PD) diagnosed many years earlier, spinal stenosis, lumbar radiculopathy status post L3-5 laminectomy, and anxiety, presented to the outpatient medical toxicology clinic due to concerns that she was being poisoned by a neighbor. She was referred by her primary care physician. The patient reported moving back to her hometown two years prior, and shortly thereafter became concerned about drug activity occurring in the neighborhood. In particular, she was concerned that one neighbor was “trying to kill me” because she had informed the police about the drug activity. She did not know the individual’s name or have a picture of him, but she had seen him riding in front of her house on a motorcycle. She had installed video cameras around her house, but believed that the neighbor had been hacking them. The patient had reported her concerns regarding being poisoned to the police on multiple occasions, but they had not found sufficient evidence to pursue further investigation. The patient reported that she noticed a “burning, sweet smelling cigar-like smell” that only occurred when she entered her home from the outside, adding that no one else smelled this. Separate from this phenomenon, at one time she noted a gas-like smell in the home and the carbon monoxide detector in the house alarmed, and she later discovered that the home had a fractured natural gas line. Following repair of the gas line the carbon monoxide detector no longer alarmed, but she continued to experience a cigar-like smell whenever she entered her home. Radon testing was negative. She also reported a pruritic scalp associated with intermittent blisters; an evaluation for varicella zoster was negative. Additional symptoms included: lightheadedness (improved with meclizine), hot and cold flashes, intermittent jerking of extremities, oily skin, chest heaviness, shortness of breath, and increased anxiety. The patient lived with her husband and dog; neither of them experienced any novel symptoms. She believed that the neighbor was poisoning her via aerosolized glyphosate that entered the home through the air conditioning and that the toxicant specifically targeted her. She further believed that she was the only member of the household affected by the toxicant because her PD made her uniquely sensitive to it. The patient provided a large packet of medical documents, including notes from emergency department and outpatient evaluations, as well as extensive laboratory and imaging work-up. How do you Evaluate Non-Specific Toxicologic Exposures in an Outpatient Setting? Managing an unknown toxicologic exposure in an ambulatory outpatient setting is very different than managing one in an emergent inpatient setting. Rapid identification of a possibly reversible toxidrome is necessary when treating the critically ill poisoned patient [ 1 ], often with clear guidelines on timely and appropriate care [ 2 ]. There is less guidance available for evaluation and management of chronic toxicologic exposures managed in the outpatient setting. Most patients present to an outpatient toxicology clinic due to concern for an environmental or occupational exposure or for the purpose of interpretation of prior laboratory testing results. Relatively few patients presenting to an outpatient toxicology clinic have symptoms related to an exposure, and relatively few patients require toxicologic treatment [ 3 , 4 ]. Patients may also present with nonspecific (“vaguely neurologic”) symptoms that they attribute to a single prior exposure that they believe to be toxic [ 5 ]. For a patient presenting to an outpatient appointment with nonspecific occupational-, environmental- or other toxicologic-related symptoms [ 6 , 7 ], we recommend a holistic, biopsychosocial evaluation including social history, mental health and substance use history, and environmental exposures. Importantly, patients with multiple somatic complaints are more likely to attribute their symptoms to an illness with an organic rather than psychological etiology [ 8 ]. We focus on excluding toxicologic red flag symptoms [ 9 ] and exposures with a known antidotal therapy [ 10 ], as management recommendations will otherwise consist of symptomatic/supportive care and reducing exposure to the presumed causative agent. It is also vital to comprehensively address the patient’s concerns regarding their symptoms, as well as their expectations for the outpatient appointment [ 11 ], and to appropriately offer further work-up, referral, or reassurance. What are Signs that Someone Might be the Target of Intentional Malicious Poisoning? It is important to consider whether or not the patient’s concern for poisoning is well-founded. Classically, victims of malicious poisonings may display few or non-specific symptoms, and available history may be incomplete or unreliable. A patient may be initially misdiagnosed, perhaps due to a clinician’s low index of suspicion for poisoning. Fatalities might be misattributed to natural causes or accident, such that a case of malicious poisoning may go undetected or be only revealed after significant forensic investigation [ 12 , 13 ]. Although the true prevalence is not fully known, intentional malicious poisoning is thought to be relatively rare, both in general and as a method of homicide [ 14 , 15 ]. Malicious poisoning victims are generally divided equally between men and women [ 12 ]. Although most malicious poisoning victims are between 25- and 44-years-old [ 12 ], the mortality rate is highest in the pediatric and geriatric populations [ 13 ]. Most victims are from outside the family of the offender [ 12 ]. Common motives for homicide include quarrels, revenge, relationship issues, robbery, or property disputes [ 14 ]. Pharmacologic properties of xenobiotics that would facilitate malicious poisoning include: delayed onset of effect, solubility in fats, and low lethal dose, among others [ 16 ]. Whereas intentional malicious poisonings were once commonly performed with arsenic, cyanide and insecticides, now they are most commonly performed with prescription medications or illicit drugs [ 13 ]. In the United States, the most common means of malicious poisoning are “drugs, medicaments and biological substances” (65%) followed by “gases and vapors” (28%) [ 15 ]; specifically, the most common poisoning agents among adults include: sympathomimetics (16%), opioids (12%), alcohol (10%), insecticides (8%), and caustics (8%) [ 17 ]. It is difficult to give specific recommendations for evaluating this rare phenomenon, but one can consider malicious poisoning if an otherwise heathy person “mysteriously worsens despite diagnosis and treatment of a suspected illness” [ 13 ], or if epidemiologic techniques indicate a common toxic source affecting a population experiencing similar symptoms [ 18 ]. Appreciating that obtaining an accurate and comprehensive history is extremely challenging in the case of malicious poisoning, if a patient’s concern for poisoning is overall unsubstantiated or not credible, then it is likely that the patient is either: misattributing symptoms to an alleged poisoning, but can be convinced of a more reasonable attribution; or else, the patient has a persecutory delusion, which is a fixed false belief resistant to contradictory evidence. Case Continuation Medical record review was notable for the following: Systolic blood pressures ranging from 140–150 mmHg over the past year. Vitals were otherwise within normal limits. Complete blood count and comprehensive metabolic panel were normal. Hemoglobin A1c was 6.1%. Serum creatinine kinase was 153 U/L (reference: 34.0–145.0 U/L). CRP was normal. TSH and free T4 were normal. Urine drug screen was negative. EKG was normal. Urine glyphosate was 4.3 μg/g creatinine (reference range 0.38 to 2.5, for 95th percentile). CT head was normal. CTA head and neck demonstrated no vascular occlusion or aneurysm, with an incidental finding of a thyroid nodule without associated cervical lymphadenopathy; follow-up thyroid ultrasound had not been performed. MRI brain without contrast demonstrated age-related brain changes without acute process. MRI L-spine demonstrated multilevel degenerative disc disease, status post laminectomy without signs of postoperative complication. Portable chest x-ray was normal. Physical exam was only notable for bilateral upper extremity resting tremor, mild upper extremity cogwheel rigidity, shuffling gait, and right foot drop. Her thought processes were linear, logical, and goal-directed. She denied any suicidal or homicidal ideation. She did not appear to be responding to internal or unseen/unheard external stimuli. What is the Differential Diagnosis for this Patient’s Symptoms? The patient’s symptoms are not consistent with a unified toxidrome. The patient is primarily concerned with glyphosate toxicity. There is controversial data suggesting an association between glyphosate exposure and the development of PD [ 19 ]. As noted previously, the patient was diagnosed with PD several years prior to her alleged exposures. Acutely, glyphosate exposure can cause skin or eye irritation; chronically, glyphosate exposure can result in oxidative stress leading to possible gastrointestinal, renal, and neuronal toxicity [ 20 ]. Although geriatric patients are generally at increased risk of adverse effects from xenobiotic exposure, there is limited data to suggest that PD specifically makes one more susceptible to glyphosate-related adverse effects. The fact that the other inhabitants of the home were not experiencing symptoms argues against an aerosolized exposure to glyphosate. Moreover, the patient’s urine glyphosate level is difficult to interpret. Most of the U.S. population ≥ 6-years-old has some exposure to glyphosate, with urine glyphosate concentrations varying throughout the day, in large part dependent on the degree of fasting prior to urine testing [ 21 ]. Outside the domain of toxicology, many of the patient’s symptoms are consistent with PD, including: olfactory dysfunction [ 22 ], seborrheic dermatitis and hyperhidrosis [ 23 ], vestibular dysfunction [ 24 ], autonomic dysfunction [ 25 ], and myoclonus [ 26 ]. Her concern for being poisoned also raises suspicion for possible PD-related persecutory delusions [ 27 ] and psychosis [ 28 ]. Either delusions, hallucinations, or disorganized speech is a necessary diagnostic criterion for psychosis. A delusion is defined as a fixed false belief, which persists despite contradictory evidence. Delusions are possible whenever a patient suffers from psychosis, but a patient can also suffer from isolated delusional disorder in the absence of other psychotic symptoms. Persecutory delusions (also known as paranoia) are among the most common delusions across all mood and thought disorders [ 29 ]. There are numerous secondary causes of psychosis, including traumatic, endocrinologic, metabolic, neurologic, and infectious, among others [ 30 , 31 ]. What Toxicants can Cause Psychosis, Including Delusions? There are various toxicants associated with psychotic symptoms (Table 1 ). Substances commonly associated with the development of psychotic symptoms include cannabinoids, ethanol, sedative-hypnotics, stimulants, opioids, hallucinogens, and psychedelics. Among patients with genetic risk for psychosis, use of these substances may result in the eventual transition from a substance-induced psychotic disorder to a primary thought disorder [ 32 – 34 ]. Table 1. Toxicants associated with the development of psychotic symptoms. Acetaminophen Amantadine Bromide Cannabinoids Carbon disulfide Carbon monoxide Corticosteroids (e.g., dexamethasone) Cyanide Cycloserine Dextromethorphan Disulfiram Dopamine agonists (e.g., levodopa, pramipexole) Efavirenz Entactogens (e.g., 3,4-methyl​enedioxy​methamphetamine [MDMA]) Ethanol Ergot alkaloids (e.g., ergotamine) Toluene Tryptamines (e.g., lysergic acid diethylamide [LSD]) Heavy metals (e.g., arsenic, bismuth, lead, manganese, mercury, thallium) Inhalants (e.g., nitrous oxide, toluene) Ketamine Nitrogen dioxide Opioids (e.g., morphine) Phencyclidine (PCP) Phenytoin Psychedelics (e.g., psilocybin) Sedative hypnotics (e.g., zolpidem, gamma-hydroxybutyric acid [GHB]) Stimulants (e.g., cocaine, amphetamine, methamphetamine) Synthetic cathinones (“bath salts”) Open in a new tab Less commonly, development of psychotic symptoms is associated with exposure to various occupational and environmental toxicants. Carbon monoxide [ 35 , 36 ] and cyanide [ 37 , 38 ] can cause delayed neuropsychological sequelae secondary to bilateral globus pallidus necrosis. The air pollutant and vehicle emission nitrogen dioxide is associated with psychosis due to oxidative stress and neuroinflammation, and this association in part may explain the higher prevalence of psychosis in urban relative to rural settings [ 39 , 40 ]. Inhalants such as nitrous oxide and toluene can result in psychosis due to the inactivation of vitamin B 12 and effects on various neuronal receptors, respectively [ 41 – 44 ]. Ergot alkaloids, an important agent in ancient religious rituals, the cause of ergotism from fungus-contaminated rye (“St. Anthony’s Fire”), and the possible cause of the Salem witchcraft affair, can result in psychosis due to serotonergic and dopaminergic mechanisms [ 45 – 48 ]. Carbon disulfide was historically important as an industrial fat solvent, and also used in the cold vulcanization of rubber and the manufacture of viscose rayon. Industrial workers exposed to carbon disulfide are at risk of psychosis due to demyelination and inhibition of dopamine beta-hydroxylase [ 49 – 51 ]. Interestingly, disulfiram was discovered due to rubber factory workers’ aversion to alcohol [ 52 ]. In part because disulfiram is metabolized into carbon disulfide, disulfiram has also been associated with psychosis [ 53 , 54 ]. Particularly in the neurodevelopmental period, excessive exposure to certain heavy metals—especially lead, manganese, arsenic, cadmium, and to a lesser extent copper—can increase the risk of developing psychosis due to various neurotransmitter disturbances [ 55 , 56 ]. Occupational exposure to manganese, particularly in welders, is a well-described cause of psychosis [ 57 ]. Thallium, a common metal used in developing countries for electronic devices and rodenticides, can result in psychosis due to mitochondrial dysfunction and inhibition of glycolysis [ 58 ]. Excessive ingestion of over-the-counter bismuth preparations to treat gastrointestinal symptoms can also lead to psychosis from bismuth toxicity [ 59 ]. There are also pharmaceutical agents associated with psychosis, including: acetaminophen [ 60 ], amantadine [ 61 ], corticosteroids [ 62 ], cycloserine [ 63 ], dopamine agonists [ 64 ], efavirenz [ 65 ], phenytoin [ 66 ], and zolpidem [ 67 ]. Use of bromide has been associated with the “normalization” of epilepsy, in which the disappearance of epileptiform discharges on EEG co-occurs with the development of psychotic symptoms [ 68 , 69 ]. Anticholinergic agents [ 70 ], as well as medications from several other pharmacologic classes have been associated with psychosis [ 71 – 73 ], but these cases occur primarily within the context of delirium. What Clinical Instruments can you Use During the Outpatient Appointment to Assess for Potential Mental Health Co-Morbidities? In addition to screening for delusions, this patient should be screened for other relevant mental health co-morbidities, as there is significant overlap between mental health disorders [ 74 ], and detection of mental health comorbidities can indicate possible etiologies for the patient’s experience or interpretation of symptoms. Patients can initially be screened for depression with a Patient Health Questionnaire-2 (PHQ-2) [ 75 ], with a positive screen indicating that a patient should be screened with a Patient Health Questionnaire-9 (PHQ-9), which measures depressive symptom severity [ 76 ]. Anxiety symptom severity can be screened for with a General Anxiety Disorder-7 (GAD-7) [ 77 ]. Somatic symptom severity can be screened with either the Somatic Symptom Scale-8 (SSS-8) [ 78 ] or the Patient Health Questionnaire-15 (PHQ-15) [ 79 ]. Two common instruments for evaluating delusions are the Brown Assessment of Beliefs Scale (BABS) [ 80 ] and 21-Item Peters et al. Delusions Inventory (PDI-21) [ 81 ]. The BABS has a possible score range of 0 to 24, and focuses primarily on evaluation of delusions; whereas, the PDI-21 has a possible score range of 0 to 336, and assesses delusions in addition to other psychotic symptoms. Patients with delusions should also be screened for cognitive impairment [ 82 ]. The Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) [ 83 ] and the Saint Louis University Mental Status exam (SLUMS) [ 84 ] are both brief questionnaires for mild cognitive impairment, and test the cognitive domains of executive function, visuospatial, memory, language, attention and orientation. Case Continuation The patient had no known prior history of psychosis. She underwent screening with the above clinical questionnaires, with scores suggestive of severe somatic symptoms and equivocal for severe delusions (Table 2 ). Table 2. Clinical instruments of interest when assessing an outpatient with concern for delusions. Clinical Instrument Outcome Patient Score Reference Range Patient Health Questionnaire-2 (PHQ-2) Depression Screening 0 Likely major depressive disorder: 3–6 Patient Health Questionnaire-9 (PHQ-9) Depression symptom severity 4 Minimal: 1–4 Mild: 5–9 Moderate: 10–14 Moderately Severe: 15–19 Severe: 20–27 General Anxiety Disorder-7 (GAD-7) Anxiety symptom severity 5 Minimal: 0–4 Mild: 5–9 Moderate: 10–14 Severe: 15–21 Somatic Symptom Scale-8 (SSS-8) Somatic symptom severity 20 Minimal: 0–3 Low: 4–7 Medium: 8–11 High: 12–15 Very High: 16–32 Patient Health Questionnaire-15: Physical Symptoms (PHQ-15) Somatic symptom severity 18 Low: 5–9 Medium: 10–14 High: 15–30 Brown Assessment of Beliefs Scale, Adult Version (BABS) Delusion severity Conviction scored as 4, and total score 13 Delusional: Conviction scored as 4, and the total score is ≥ 18/24 21-Item Peters et al. Delusions Inventory (PDI-21) Delusion severity Yes/No: 5 Distress: 17 Preoccupation: 15 Conviction: 15 Total: 52 Healthy: mean (SD) Yes/No: 6.7 (4.4) Distress: 15.5 (14.1) Preoccupation: 15.4 (14.1) Conviction: 20.4 (16.0) Total: 58.9 (48.0) Deluded: mean (SD) Yes/No: 11.9 (6.0) Distress: 36.7 (23.6) Preoccupation: 36.1 (24.7) Conviction: 44.5 (27.4) Total: 130.5 (79.1) Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) Mild Cognitive Impairment 26 Normal: ≥ 26/30 Saint Louis University Mental Status exam (SLUMS) Neurocognitive Disorder Severity 26 High school education Normal: 27–30 Mild Neurocognitive Disorder: 21–26 Dementia: 1–20 Less than high school education Normal: 25–30 Mild Neurocognitive Disorder: 20–24 Dementia: 1–19 Open in a new tab The patient reported medication adherence. Her prescription medications included: aspirin 81 mg daily; carbidopa/levodopa 25 mg/100 mg, two tablets QID and two tablets at night if awakened by dystonia; fenofibrate 200 mg at night; metformin 1000 mg BID; pramipexole 0.25 mg TID; and, rasagiline 1 mg daily. Over the past year the patient also had a two-month trial of entacapone 200 mg TID, without improvement in PD symptoms or change in concerns regarding being poisoned; this medication was discontinued several months prior to presentation. The patient’s long-term clonazepam prescription had also been discontinued within the past few months. Additionally, the patient took acetaminophen and topical diclofenac gel as needed for pain, and meclizine as need for vertigo. She also took the following supplements: multivitamin daily; omega-3 fatty acid supplement daily; vitamin D 3 daily; and, vitamin E daily. She denied any use of additional supplements, including other other-the-counter or herbal medications. She denied any use of tobacco, alcohol, cannabinoids, methamphetamine, cocaine, opioids or hallucinogens. Her other outpatient medical providers included a primary care physician, neurologist and cardiologist. She had also been referred to a psychiatrist, but had not yet scheduled an appointment. How Could Medications Used to Manage PD Contribute to Delusions or Psychosis? Delusions are among the most common psychotic symptoms that can develop in PD [ 85 ]. The neurobiology underlying delusions is incompletely understood, but is based in the limbic system (which includes the amygdala and hippocampus, among other brain regions) [ 86 ]. Delusions also arise due to dopaminergic and serotonergic dysfunction [ 87 ]. Dopamine agonist medications used to manage the motor symptoms of PD can worsen Parkinson’s-related psychotic symptoms, in part by overstimulating dopaminergic neurons in the mesolimbic pathway [ 88 ]. Psychotic symptoms occur in about 10% of patients with PD who are not treated with dopamine agonists, but occur in about 40% of patients with PD who are treated with dopamine agonists [ 64 ]. The development of psychosis in PD is associated with a dose-dependent relationship to dopaminergic medications [ 89 , 90 ]. Therefore, the initial management for psychosis in the setting of antiparkinsonian medication is to attempt to reduce the dose of pro-dopaminergic agents. Among patients with PD who develop psychosis related to dopamine-agonist medication, medication dose reduction can lead to remission of psychosis in about 60% of cases, but is also associated with worsening of motor symptoms [ 91 ]. If psychotic symptoms persist despite medication dose alterations, or if reduction in dopamine agonists results in intolerable motor symptoms, then the addition of antipsychotic medication may be necessary [ 92 ]. How do you Evaluate and Manage Persecutory Delusions? In order to determine whether a patient’s belief is delusional, the provider must first appreciate to what extent the belief is unfounded, not credible, false or irrational, taking into account the patient’s education, social/cultural context, and religious beliefs. The provider must also consider to what extent the delusion is dysfunctional [ 93 ]. Review of past medical records and interviews with collateral sources can be immensely helpful in clarifying and supplementing the patient’s history [ 94 , 95 ]. Delusions are frequently among the most refractory psychotic symptoms [ 96 ]. Persecutory delusions are in part grounded in: anxious or negatively-biased cognitive frameworks that color otherwise insignificant or ambiguous events; interpersonal sensitivity; and, certain cognitive biases that preclude consideration of alternative explanations [ 97 , 98 ]. It is not uncommon for toxicologists to evaluate patients who have unfounded concerns that they are being poisoned [ 99 ]. Patients who present to the outpatient toxicology clinic due to concerns about potential poisoning may suffer from a thought disorder [ 100 ], delusional disorder-persecutory type, or some degree of overvalued beliefs [ 101 ]. In these cases, providers must screen for any plans the patient might have to commit any violence against the person whom the patient alleges to be poisoning them, which the patient might justify as a preemptive act to secure their own safety [ 102 ]. The management of patients’ delusions and co-occurring issues often necessitates collaborative and multidisciplinary care [ 103 ]. In addition to identifying, discontinuing or reducing exposure to any causative toxicants, the mainstay of pharmacologic management of delusions is antipsychotics, similar to the treatment of other psychotic disorders [ 104 , 105 ]. Non-pharmacologic management strategies include: building rapport; allowing the patient to disclose the content of the delusion and the evidence for its belief; eliciting the patient''s emotions and distress associated with the delusions; identifying any association between the delusion and dysfunctional behavior; identifying stressors; assessing the potential for harm to self or others associated with the delusion; educating the patient on self-soothing and coping mechanisms; improving interpersonal dynamics; and, improving sleep [ 106 – 108 ]. Confronting a patient about a delusion is generally ill-advised until the provider has developed rapport and the patient has demonstrated an ability to meta-cognate about their beliefs [ 109 , 110 ]. How do you Best Manage Patients Who Present to the Outpatient Toxicology Clinic Who do not have a Primary Underlying Toxicologic Syndrome? Many patients present to an outpatient toxicology clinic due to an alleged exposure to an unknown toxicant that they believe to be causing symptoms [ 111 ]. Some of these patients might be diagnosed with idiopathic environmental intolerance (EI) or multiple chemical sensitivity (MCS) [ 112 – 115 ], while also meeting criteria for somatic symptom disorder [ 116 – 118 ], or a co-occurring depressive, anxiety, or trauma or stressor-related disorder [ 119 , 120 ]. For these patients, psychological distress or classical conditioning may result in physical and somatic complaints [ 121 – 123 ] that they attribute to chemical exposures [ 124 – 126 ]. There are several therapeutic approaches to caring for patients with somatic and medically-unexplained symptoms. If the patient has a prior laboratory test indicating a chemical exposure, acknowledge that the patient might have had a chemical exposure but that exposure is not determinative of chemical toxicity [ 127 ]. After excluding underlying toxicologic and medical causes of the symptoms, providers should also exclude co-occurring mental health disorders. Acknowledge the patient’s symptoms and the pain/distress they are causing. Reassure the patient that a thorough work-up has been performed, and a medical explanation has not been found. Empathize with the patient in their frustration, but also reassure the patient that several worrisome diagnoses have been excluded. Manage treatment expectations: acknowledge that an underlying medical etiology could not be found at this time, and that it is possible that an association between a chemical exposure and the patient’s syndrome may be found retroactively, but that symptomatic relief in the present is possible. Focus on reducing distress, improving functionality and lifestyle, and appropriately reducing unnecessary healthcare utilization (thereby reducing financial strain and the risk of iatrogenic adverse effects) [ 128 , 129 ]. The patient’s treatment should be centralized, often through a primary care physician, who can provide scheduled follow-ups at regular intervals for monitoring and provision of symptomatic/supportive care [ 130 ]. The primary pharmacologic management of somatic symptom disorder is an antidepressant [ 131 ]. Various psychotherapy models are also beneficial [ 132 ]. While a patient with primarily somatic complaints may greatly benefit from the supportive relationship of a physician who demonstrates an interest in alleviating the patient’s distressing symptoms, patients who present with a diagnosis of EI/MCS or who are committed to the idea of a chemical/environmental etiology may not be reassured by any explanation that contradicts their pre-conceived model of symptom causation [ 133 ]. These patients may seek vindication for their beliefs (i.e., proving that they are correct about the cause of their symptoms), leveraging confirmation bias and other cognitive biases to that end. The role of being ill secondary to a chemical exposure may become central to their sense of self. They may also believe providers who counsel them against their ideas to be adversarial [ 124 , 134 , 135 ]. These patients may have a paranoid or obsessive–compulsive personality style [ 136 ], in which case providers have the difficult task of counseling patients about the medically-unexplained nature of their symptoms, while also being consistent and detailed in that counseling, and avoiding confrontation, conflict, and defensiveness [ 137 ]. With education and psychotherapy, these patients can have some degree of improvement in symptoms and lifestyle [ 138 ]; however, many will continue to be impacted by medically unexplained symptoms that they attribute to chemical exposures [ 139 , 140 ]. Disagreement between patients and providers over the causation of the symptoms may erode the patient-physician relationship [ 125 ]. Multidisciplinary collaboration with psychiatric providers can be helpful in managing these challenging cases [ 141 ], at least in part to explore the relationship of the patient’s symptoms following an alleged exposure with the significance of their psychological response to that exposure [ 142 ]. Case Conclusion The patient was counseled that her history, symptoms, and physical exam were not consistent with an acute chemical exposure. The fact that her belief regarding being poisoned was rigidly refractory to contradictory evidence, in the absence of other psychotic symptoms, suggested that she suffered from delusional disorder, persecutory type. The patient was on several pro-dopaminergic medications for PD (carbidopa/levodopa, pramipexole, and rasagiline), raising suspicion that there could have been a pharmacologic contribution to her delusion. On follow-up several months later, the patient continued to believe that she was being poisoned by her neighbor, but she had no plan to harm him. She reported that she did not talk to others about her concerns because she believed that it “makes people feel uncomfortable” and was worried that others would think she is “nuts,” but added that her concerns “would unnerve me too” if she were in their position. The patient had no interim medication or dosage changes since the initial outpatient medical toxicology appointment. She had followed-up with her neurologist, who counseled her that some of her symptoms that she attributed to the alleged poisoning were instead attributable to her PD. However, the patient was “still under the impression” that her symptoms were due to the alleged poison. She had not yet seen a psychiatrist, but had an appointment scheduled within the coming months. In the interim period the patient had submitted urine and hair samples for heavy metal testing by a private company, which returned without any results outside of the company’s reference ranges. The patient stated that by this test “I ruled out one thing [and] I’ve done it on my own,” but she continued to believe that some as-yet-undetected toxicant was poisoning her. Her husband had died of complications of his pre-existing conditions, and the patient was living alone with support from nearby family. Due to ongoing concern for being poisoned by her neighbor, she planned to sell her house and move. Acknowledgements No organizations or grants funded this work. Sources of Funding No authors have funding sources, disclosures, or potential conflicts of interest pertaining to this submission. Declarations Author Agreement All individuals who are included as authors have approved this version of the manuscript. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Parris MA, Calello DP. Found Down: Approach to the Patient with an Unknown Poisoning. Emerg Med Clin North Am. 2022;40(2):193–222. doi: 10.1016/j.emc.2022.01.011. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Lavonas EJ, Akpunonu PD, Arens AM, Babu KM, Cao D, Hoffman RS, Hoyte CO, Mazer-Amirshahi ME, Stolbach A, St-Onge M, Thompson TM, Wang GS, Hoover AV, Drennan IR, American Heart Association American Heart Association focused update on the management of patients with cardiac arrest or life-threatening toxicity due to poisoning: an update to the american heart association guidelines for cardiopulmonary resuscitation and emergency cardiovascular care. Circulation. 2023;148(16):e149–e184. doi: 10.1161/CIR.0000000000001161. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Farrugia LA, Rhyee SH, On behalf of the ACMT Toxicology Investigators Consortium (ToxIC). Characteristics of Patients Presenting to a Medical Toxicologist f