INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4495,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8165900','Compressed sensing time-of-flight magnetic resonance angiography with high spatial resolution for evaluating intracranial aneurysms: comparison with digital subtraction angiography.','Kim D, Heo YJ, Jeong HW, Baek JW, Shin GW, Jin SC, Baek HJ, Ryu KH, Kim KS, Kim I.','2021','incoherently steno autocalibrating incoherently exploits sparsity icas bifurcations embolisation biplane integris artis skyra slabs slabs thresholding fista inline fista completes slab aliasing aliasing aliasing overdiagnosed mariakerke saccular paraclinoid junctional paraclinoid steno paraclinoid steno sharper fushimi regularisation sparsity ringing starry sky streaky equalised inje hirabuki oelerich lentschig zunker monninghoff maderwald theysohn rofo bhagat autocalibrating preibisch wallenhorst heidemann autocalibrating msense akcakaya basha millimeter lustig donoho pauly uecker frahm undersampled undersampling stalder undersampled hutter undersampled magma lustig donoho pauly steno fushimi undersampling e3774 fushimi e0189493 fushimi undersampling rooij sprengers gast debrey belliveau michaely piccini greve sollmann hock sartoretti reischauer sartoretti tzung okahara kiyosue brinjikji lanzino','Abstract Background and purpose Compressed sensing is used for accelerated acquisitions with incoherently under-sampled k-space data, and intracranial time-of-flight magnetic resonance angiography is suitable for compressed sensing. Compressed sensing time-of-flight is beneficial in decreasing acquisition time and increasing spatial resolution while maintaining acquisition time. In this retrospective study, we aimed to evaluate the image quality and diagnostic performance of compressed sensing time-of-flight with high spatial resolution and compare with parallel imaging time-of-flight using digital subtraction angiography as a reference. Material and methods In total, 39 patients with 46 intracranial aneurysms underwent parallel imaging and compressed sensing time-of-flight in the same imaging session and digital subtraction angiography before or after magnetic resonance angiography. The overall image quality, artefacts and diagnostic confidence were assessed by two observers. The contrast ratio, maximal aneurysm diameters and diagnostic performance were evaluated. Results Compressed sensing time-of-flight showed significantly better overall image quality, degree of artefacts and diagnostic confidence in both observers, with better inter-observer agreement. The contrast ratio was significantly higher for compressed sensing time-of-flight than for parallel imaging time-of-flight in both observers (source images, P < 0.001; maximum intensity projection images, P < 0.05 for both observers); however, the measured maximal diameters of aneurysms were not significantly different. Compressed sensing time-of-flight showed higher sensitivity, specificity, accuracy and positive and negative predictive values for detecting aneurysms than parallel imaging time-of-flight in both observers, with better inter-observer agreement. Compressed sensing time-of-flight was preferred over parallel imaging time-of-flight by both observers; however, parallel imaging time-of-flight was preferred in cases of giant and large aneurysms. Conclusions Compressed sensing-time-of-flight provides better image quality and diagnostic performance than parallel imaging time-of-flight. However, neuroradiologists should be aware of under-sampling artefacts caused by compressed sensing. Keywords: Compressed sensing, time-of-flight magnetic resonance angiography, cerebral aneurysm Introduction Time-of-flight (TOF) magnetic resonance angiography (MRA) is a reliable method to evaluate intracranial steno-occlusive lesions 1 , 2 or aneurysms. 3 , 4 It is widely used to evaluate cerebrovascular disease because it is non-invasive with no need for exogenous contrast media. However, it is difficult to perform TOF-MRA with high spatial resolution to achieve detailed vascular depiction because it requires a longer scanning time than conventional TOF-MRA. Scanning time reduction is important for high spatial resolution and for reducing motion artefacts, which could decrease the resolution of vascular structures. Parallel imaging (PI), such as generalised autocalibrating partially parallel acquisition (GRAPPA) or sensitivity encoding (SENSE), has been used to reduce scanning time by under-sampling in the phase-encoding direction. However, the acceleration factor of PI-TOF should remain at two to three-fold because higher acceleration factors cause a decrease in the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) associated with the geometry factor. 5 , 6 Compressed sensing (CS), an alternative image reconstruction technique, has recently been used for accelerated acquisitions with incoherently under-sampled k-space data and exploits the sparsity of the image in a transform domain. 7 – 9 Intracranial TOF-MRA is suitable for CS because high-signal arteries are sparse in space. 10 – 13 CS-TOF decreases acquisition time and increases spatial resolution while maintaining acquisition time. Numerous studies have applied CS to TOF-MRA in order to reduce scanning time 14 – 16 or focus on sequence optimisation. 17 – 19 However, we used CS-TOF to increase spatial resolution while maintaining acquisition time. Although some studies 17 , 19 have used CS-TOF in patients with intracranial aneurysms, they focused on sequence optimisation and PI-TOF comparison. To our knowledge, there are no previous studies comparing CS-TOF with digital subtraction angiography (DSA). Therefore, we evaluated the image quality and diagnostic performance of CS-TOF compared to PI-TOF using DSA) as a reference for the depiction of intracranial aneurysms. Methods Study population Between June 2019 and December 2019, 222 patients with suspected intracranial artery disease underwent PI-TOF and CS-TOF MRA in the same imaging session. We included patients with at least one intracranial aneurysm confirmed on DSA before or after MRA. We excluded: (a) patients with vascular lesions other than aneurysms, such as stenosis ( n = 114); (b) patients without any vascular lesions ( n = 44); and (c) patients with intracranial aneurysms who did not undergo DSA ( n = 25). Finally, we enrolled 39 patients (15 men and 24 women; mean age, 60.1 ± 9.30 years) with 46 intracranial aneurysms (27 distal internal carotid arteries (ICAs), eight middle cerebral artery (MCA) bifurcations, seven distal vertebral arteries, two basilar arteries, one anterior communicating artery, one posterior cerebral artery). Six patients had two aneurysms and one patient had three. Four patients presented with stenosis at other intracranial arteries and four patients previously had coil embolisation for aneurysms in other intracranial arteries. The local institutional review board approved this study and the requirement for obtaining written informed consent was waived due to the retrospective study design. Cerebral angiography was performed in all patients, before or after performing both types of MRA using a biplane angiographic unit (Integris V, Philips, Amsterdam, The Netherlands; or Artis zee, Siemens Healthineers, Erlangen, Germany), except in one patient who underwent cerebral angiography before MRA outside the hospital. The average interval between the latest DSA and MRA was 18.2 ± 18.9 months (range one day to 69 months). MRA parameters All studies were performed using a 3T magnetic resonance scanner (Skyra, Siemens Healthineers) with a 64-channel head coil. A research sequence and reconstruction prototype provided by Siemens Healthineers were used for CS-TOF and detailed parameters were as follows: field of view (FOV), 220 × 220; repetition time/echo time (TR/TE), 17.6 s/3.7 s; flip angle, 20°; matrix, 368 × 368; slice thickness, 0.4 mm; number of slabs, six (slice oversampling, 14.3%); acceleration method, compressed sensing; acceleration factor, 4.6; reconstructed voxel size, 0.4 × 0.4 × 0.4 mm 3 ; number of slices, 236; scan time, 5 min 29 s. For PI-TOF, the parameters were as follows: FOV, 220 × 177.2; TR/TE, 24 s/3.99 s; flip angle, 20°; matrix, 576 × 302; slice thickness, 0.6 mm; number of slabs, six (slice oversampling, 11.1%); acceleration method, GRAPPA; acceleration factor, 2; reconstructed voxel size, 0.4 × 0.4 × 0.6 mm 3 ; number of slices, 171; scan time, 6 min 42 s. All patients underwent CS-TOF followed by PI-TOF in the same session. After data acquisition, images were reconstructed using eight iterations of the modified fast iterative shrinkage-thresholding algorithm (FISTA) (18) with a regularization factor of 0.0015. The CS-TOF reconstruction was implemented inline on the scanner and started at the end of image acquisition. The current implementation of FISTA completes the reconstruction in 1 min 10 s per slab (total: 7 minutes). PI-TOF was reconstructed with the scanner’s implementation of GRAPPA. Image analysis Two neuroradiologists with 6 and 2 years of experience in neuroimaging (respectively) independently evaluated source images and maximum intensity projection (MIP) images of CS-TOF and PI-TOF and were blind to the acquisition technique and patient information. The two neuroradiologists interpreted MRA without any information regarding lesion location or number. For qualitative analysis, overall image quality was assessed using a 5-point scale (5: excellent quality and sharp border of vessels similar to DSA; 4: good quality and clear border, but somewhat reduced quality compared with DSA; 3: moderate quality with visible border, but sufficient for diagnosis; 2: limited quality with blurred border of vessel; 1: unclear border, non-diagnostic). Artefacts and diagnostic confidence for aneurysms were also assessed using 5-point scales (artefact; 5: no artefact, 4: slight aliasing/blurring artefact, 3: a few aliasing/blurring artefact, 2: some aliasing/blurring, one: severe artefact) (diagnostic confidence; 5: very certain (excellent), 4: diagnosis relatively certain, 3: unclear diagnosis, 2: uncertain diagnosis, 1: non-diagnostic). For quantitative analysis, we calculated the contrast ratio (CR) according to the method proposed by Tang et al. 20 All regions of interest (ROIs) were placed by one neuroradiologist for consistency. 20 CR = ( SI M1 segment of MCA – SI background ) / ( SI M1 segment of MCA + SI background ) . SI denotes the mean signal intensity. We placed ROIs in the same position on PI-TOF and CS-TOF: MCA (proximal M1 segment) and static background tissue for MIP images and MCA (proximal M1 segment) and the same level of the adjacent tissue for source images. The sizes of ROIs varied according to vessel size, but we tried to maximise them as much as possible without including voxels of other tissue. We also measured maximal diameters of aneurysms in MIP images of PI-TOF and CS-TOF MRA and three-dimensional (3D) rotational angiography (RA) of cerebral angiography. To evaluate diagnostic performance, two observers independently evaluated the presence and number of aneurysms using two types of MRA at 2-week intervals and were blinded to the DSA results. For patient-based analysis, patients with one or more aneurysms were considered positive, and others with no detected aneurysms or any other intracranial vascular disease were considered negative. When patients had multiple aneurysms and one of them was missed or overdiagnosed, we considered them as false-negative or false-positive cases during patient-based analysis. Observers were then asked to view MIP images of both types of TOF-MRA simultaneously, indicate which type was more preferred (CS-TOF, equivalent, PI-TOF) based on the delineation of cerebral arteries, intracranial aneurysms, and accompanying pathological vascular lesions (occlusion status for coiled aneurysms, stenosis), and note which was more similar to DSA. Statistical analysis Continuous variables are presented as means ± standard deviations and were assessed for normality using the Kolmogorov–Smirnov test. Overall image quality, artefacts, and diagnostic confidence of PI and CS-TOF were compared using the Wilcoxon signed-rank test. The CRs of PI and CS-TOF were compared using a paired-sample t- test if the data were normally distributed, or the Wilcoxon signed-rank test if not. In assessing the size of aneurysms, repeated-measures analysis of variance (ANOVA) was used if the data were normally distributed or the Kruskal–Wallis test if not. A P value of less than 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Cohen’s kappa was used for inter-observer agreement of overall image quality, artefacts, diagnostic confidence and detection of aneurysms, and the intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) was calculated to quantify inter-observer reproducibility for the contrast ratio as follows: no agreement (0), slight agreement (0.01–0.20), fair agreement (0.21–0.40), moderate agreement (0.41–0.60), substantial agreement (0.61–0.80) and almost perfect agreement (0.81–1.00). 21 The diagnostic performance for the detection of aneurysms (sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value (PPV), and negative predictive value (NPV)) was evaluated on a per-patient and per-aneurysm basis. All statistical analyses were performed using SPSS software (version 24.0; IBM, Armonk, NY, USA) and MedCalc for Windows (version 18.11.3; MedCalc Software, Mariakerke, Belgium). Results The overall image quality of CS-TOF was significantly better than that of PI-TOF, according to both observers ( Table 1 ). Most CS-TOF images (64.1% in observer 1, 71.8% in observer 2) showed excellent image quality, and most PI-TOF images showed good quality (59.0% in observer 1, 51.3% in observer 2). Four (10.3%) PI-TOF images showed limited image quality, but only two (5.13%) CS-TOF images showed limited image quality. The degree of artefacts in CS-TOF was also significantly better than in PI-TOF according to both observers. The inter-observer agreement for overall image quality and degree of artefacts was substantial for PI-TOF (κ = 0.78 and 0.66, respectively) and CS-TOF (κ = 0.81 and 0.80, respectively). The diagnostic confidence of CS-TOF was significantly better than that of PI-TOF in both observers ( Figure 1 ). More CS-TOF images showed excellent diagnostic confidence (79.5% in observer 1, 76.9% in observer 2) compared to PI-TOF (61.5% in observer 1, 46.2% in observer 2). The inter-observer agreement for diagnostic confidence was substantial for PI-TOF (κ = 0.65) and almost perfect for CS-TOF (κ = 0.82). Table 1. Comparison between PI-TOF and CS-TOF for evaluation of intracranial aneurysms. Variables PI-TOF CS-TOF P value Overall image quality Observer 1 3.90 ± 0.85 4.49 ± 0.82 <0.001 Observer 2 3.95 ± 0.97 4.54 ± 0.97 <0.001 Cohen’s kappa 0.78 (95% CI: 0.63–0.93) 0.81 (95% CI: 0.65–0.96) Artefact Observer 1 3.95 ± 0.89 4.46 ± 0.85 0.002 Observer 2 4.00 ± 0.86 4.64 ± 0.84 <0.001 Cohen’s kappa 0.66 (95% CI: 0.43–0.88) 0.80 (95% CI: 0.65–0.96) Diagnostic confidence Observer 1 4.46 ± 0.79 4.72 ± 0.61 0.039 Observer 2 4.36 ± 0.67 4.69 ± 0.61 0.001 Cohen’s kappa 0.65 (95% CI: 0.48–0.84) 0.82 (95% CI: 0.64–1.00) Contrast ratio Observer 1 Source images 0.66 ± 0.46 0.69 ± 0.57* <0.001 MIP images 0.57 ± 0.06 0.59 ± 0.07** 0.017 Observer 2 Source images 0.68 ± 0.45 0.72 ± 0.04* <0.001 MIP images 0.59 ± 0.57 0.61 ± 0.08** 0.018 Open in a new tab The table shows the inter-observer agreement measured by quadratic Cohen’s kappa. CI: confidence interval; CS: compressed sensing; MIP: maximum intensity projection; PI: parallel imaging; TOF: time-of-flight. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Maximum intensity projections and digital subtraction angiography (DSA) of a 48-year-old patient. Small saccular aneurysms at the right paraclinoid internal carotid artery and junctional dilation at the left posterior communicating artery are clearer on compressed sensing time-of-flight (TOF) (a, b) than parallel imaging TOF (c, d) and are more similar to DSA (e, f). The CRs of CS-TOF were significantly higher than those of PI-TOF in both observers (source images, P < 0.001 for observer 1; MIP images, P = 0.017 for observer 1, source images, P < 0.001 for observer 2; MIP images, P = 0.018 for observer 2). The mean maximal diameters of aneurysms were largest in 3D-RA (5.37 ± 5.51 mm), followed by PI-TOF (5.09 ± 5.16 mm) and CS-TOF (5.08 ± 5.19 mm). However, there were no significant differences in measured data between 3D-RA, PI-TOF and CS-TOF ( P = 0.152). The sensitivity, specificity, accuracy, PPV and NPV of PI and CS-TOF are summarised in Table 2 . Inter-observer agreements for CS-TOF were higher than those of PI-TOF on both aneurysm-based analysis (κ = 0.92 for PI (95% confidence interval: 0.87–0.97) and 0.98 for CS-TOF (95% confidence interval: 0.95–1.00)) and patient-based analysis (κ = 0.74 for PI (95% confidence interval: 0.57–0.90) and 0.93 for CS-TOF (95% confidence interval: 0.84–1.00)). Table 2. Diagnostic performance of PI-TOF and CS-TOF in aneurysm and patient-based evaluation. PI-TOF CS-TOF Diagnostic performance Aneurysm-based evaluation Observer 1 Sensitivity 95.00 (83.00, 99.39) 97.62 (87.43, 99.94) Specificity 87.27 (75.52, 94.73) 90.56 (79.34, 96.87) Accuracy 90.53 (82.78, 95.58) 93.68 (86.76, 97.65) PPV 84.44 (73.03, 91.59) 89.13 (78.05, 94.98) NPV 96.00 (86.10, 98.94) 97.96 (87.36, 99.70) Observer 2 Sensitivity 92.68 (80.08, 98.46) 95.45 (84.53, 99.44) Specificity 86.54 (74.21, 94.41) 92.31 (81.46, 97.86) Accuracy 89.25 (81.11, 94.72) 93.75 (86.89, 97.67) PPV 84.44 (73.05, 91.58) 91.30 (80.34, 96.43) NPV 93.75 (83.39, 97.82) 96.00 (86.08, 98.94) Patient-based evaluation Observer 1 Sensitivity 94.12 (88.32, 99.28) 97.22 (85.47, 99.93) Specificity 88.89 (77.37, 95.81) 92.31 (81.46, 97.86) Accuracy 90.91 (82.87, 95.99) 94.32 (87.24, 98.13) PPV 84.21 (71.40, 91.93) 89.74 (77.31, 95.74) NPV 96.00 (86.18, 98.93) 97.96 (87.40, 99.70) Observer 2 Sensitivity 91.67 (77.53, 98.25) 94.59 (81.81, 99.34) Specificity 86.54 (74.21, 94.41) 94.12 (83.76, 98.77) Accuracy 88.64 (80.09, 94.41) 94.32 (87.24, 98.13) PPV 82.50 (70.15, 90.44) 92.11 (79.51, 97.23) NPV 93.75 (83.47, 97.80) 96.00 (86.16, 98.93) Open in a new tab Data are percentages; data in parentheses are 95% confidence intervals. CS: compressed sensing; PI: parallel imaging; PPV: positive predictive value; NPV: negative predictive value; TOF: time-of-flight. CS-TOF images were preferred and were more similar to DSA in 20 cases (51.3%) according to observer 1 and in 13 cases (33.3%) according to observer 2. However, PI-TOF images were preferred in one case (2.56%) by observer 1 and in two cases (5.13%) by observer 2. These cases were large and giant aneurysms which presented with decreased blood flow of distal branches of affected arteries ( Figures 2 and 3 ). For other cases, PI-TOF and CS-TOF were considered equivalent by both observers. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Maximum intensity projections and digital subtraction angiography (DSA) of a 71-year-old patient. A giant aneurysm was located at the left paraclinoid internal carotid artery. Compressed sensing time-of-flight (TOF; a) shows decreased blood flow of the left distal M1 and M2 segments, but parallel imaging TOF (b) shows relatively well-visualised blood flow of the left distal M1 and M2 segments. There was no significant steno-occlusive lesion at the left distal M1 or M2 segments on DSA (c). Figure 3. Open in a new tab Maximum intensity projections and digital subtraction angiography (DSA) of a 60-year-old patient. Large aneurysms are located at the left paraclinoid internal carotid artery and basilar artery. Compressed sensing time-of-flight (TOF; a) shows decreased blood flow of both distal M1 and M2, but parallel imaging TOF (b) shows relatively better blood flow of both the M2 segments. There was no significant steno-occlusive lesion at the left distal M1 and M2 segments on DSA (c). Discussion In this study, we evaluated the diagnostic performance of CS-TOF for intracranial aneurysms compared to PI-TOF, using DSA and 3D-RA as references. Previous studies 10 , 14 reported that CS-TOF showed better diagnostic performance in the diagnosis of intracranial artery disease and variations than conventional TOF-MRA, but there are no previous reports comparing CS-TOF with DSA/3D-RA, which are considered standard references for assessing intracranial vascular diseases. 22 , 23 Our results showed that CS-TOF provided sharper visualisation of intracranial arteries, which is consistent with previous studies. 10 , 15 , 16 Fushimi et al. 19 also used CS-TOF to evaluate intracranial aneurysms, but they mainly focused on sequence optimisation. We assessed the diagnostic performance of CS-TOF in evaluating intracranial aneurysms, and our results showed that CS-TOF could provide a more confident diagnosis and better diagnostic performance than PI-TOF without a significant difference in aneurysm size measurement. High spatial resolution is helpful in detecting small vessel disease and in planning treatment but is difficult to achieve with TOF-MRA due to its prolonged acquisition time. PI techniques were developed and have been used to reduce scanning time. However, PI with higher acceleration factors results in significant SNR loss. 24 , 25 Conversely, CS-TOF can use a higher acceleration factor while preserving the SNR. CS techniques have been applied to magnetic resonance examinations of various body parts, 8 , 11 , 12 , 26 but they have not been widely used in clinical practice due to their long reconstruction time. We sequentially performed CS-TOF and PI-TOF, with the latter being simultaneously performed during the reconstruction of CS-TOF; thus, there was no additional time consumption. In this study, we decreased the slice thickness of CS-TOF compared to PI-TOF (0.4 mm vs. 0.6 mm) and increased the number of slices (236 slices vs. 171 slices). Despite these changes, we were able to decrease the acquisition time from 6 min 42 s to 5 min 29 s (18.2% reduction) using CS-TOF. The image quality of CS-TOF was better than that of PI-TOF, and this result was consistent with that of previous studies, 11 , 15 , 20 which reported that this may result from sparse under-sampling and iterative reconstruction. The improved contrast of small vessels using an optimised regularisation weighting factor enables better diagnoses of stenosis and occlusion of small vessels. 11 Yamamoto et al. 10 also reported that the contrast-to-background deviation of CS-TOF is higher than that of PI-TOF, and they suggested that this may be because iterative reconstruction reduces noise-like features. In our study, there were no significant differences in measured aneurysm sizes between 3D-RA, PI-TOF and CS-TOF. This is also consistent with previous studies, 15 , 17 , 27 and suggests the absence of erroneous measurement due to irregular under-sampling with CS-TOF. In our study, PI-TOF was preferred over CS-TOF in two cases and was found to be more similar to DSA ( Figures 2 and 3 ). Both of these cases were large/giant aneurysms and appeared as loss of blood flow, distal to the large/giant aneurysm. This phenomenon may reflect under-sampling artefacts caused by CS; 13 , 30 however, it is still difficult to conclude because the reconstructed matrix was not identical in both methods. Therefore, further study with giant aneurysms is required for confirmation. CS reconstructions of under-sampled measurements generate the missing data based on imaging sparsity. 10 Thus, the artefacts caused by CS are unpredictable and unfamiliar. To our knowledge, only a few studies have reported on artefacts in brain imaging using CS, 11 , 28 , 29 especially artefacts encountered in CS-TOF. 10 Global ringing artefacts and image blurring are frequently observed, and starry-sky artefacts, streaky-linear artefacts, and wax-layer artefacts have also been reported with compressed SENSE brain imaging. 28 These artefacts were caused by k-space under-sampling, 10 but they did not, however, interfere with the diagnosis. This study had several limitations. First, this was a retrospective study, and there was inevitable selection bias. Second, we could not match PI-TOF and CS-TOF parameters. We set the lowest TR value allowed by the magnetic resonance scanner for both types of TOF-MRA. Considering the artefacts that occur independently in these two types of MRA, we matched the reconstructive pixel resolution instead of the FOV for both MRA techniques. In addition, TE could not be equalised, as the frequency encoding resolution of PI-TOF is higher than that of CS-TOF. Thus, we tried to acquire images as close as possible from the opposed TE with the two types of MRA. Further prospective studies matching the parameters of both types of MRA are needed. Third, we did not compare CS-TOF with conventional TOF-MRA. PI-TOF has been widely used in clinical practice instead of conventional TOF-MRA in recent years. Thus, a comparison with PI-TOF is adequate for demonstrating the diagnostic performance of CS-TOF in clinical practice. Fourth, there were only two large/giant aneurysms that differed from DSA, but further studies with more cases are needed to validate the diagnostic performance of CS-TOF. Fifth, the average interval between the latest DSA and MRA was too long. After being initially diagnosed with DSA, untreated intracranial aneurysms were often followed up by MRA in our hospital due to their invasiveness. Furthermore, according to a previous meta-analysis, 31 the annual aneurysm growth rate was reported to be only approximately 1.5–2.5% of aneurysm volume per year, and thus it is not expected to make a significant difference. Sixth, a previous study 19 recommended 10 iterations, but we performed eight iterations due to the long reconstruction time. In conclusion, CS-TOF permits an increase in spatial resolution while preserving scanning time. However, CS-TOF may present under-sampling artefacts that could mimic pathological lesions. Therefore, neuroradiologists should be aware of artefacts caused by CS when diagnosing patients. Footnotes Author contributions: The corresponding author declares that all listed authors have fulfilled all four requirements for authorship according to international rules. Conflict of interest: The author(s) declared no potential conflicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article. Funding: The author(s) disclosed receipt of the following financial support for the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article: This work was supported by a 2019 grant from Inje University. ORCID iD: Young Jin Heo https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4765-0727 References 1. Fujita N, Hirabuki N, Fujii K, et al . MR imaging of middle cerebral artery stenosis and occlusion: value of MR angiography. AJNR Am J Neuroradiol 1994; 15: 335–341. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Oelerich M, Lentschig M, Zunker P, et al. Intracranial vascular stenosis and occlusion: comparison of 3D time-of-flight and 3D phase-contrast MR angiography. Neuroradiology 1998; 40: 567–573. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Mönninghoff C, Maderwald S, Theysohn J, et al. Evaluation of intracranial aneurysms with 7 T versus 1.5 T time-of-flight MR angiography – initial experience. Rofo 2009; 181(1): 16–23. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 4. Li M-H, Li Y-D, Tan H-Q, et al. Contrast-free MRA at 3.0 T for the detection of intracranial aneurysms. Neurology 2011; 77: 667–676. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Bhagat Y, Emery D, Naik S, et al. Comparison of generalized autocalibrating partially parallel acquisitions and modified sensitivity encoding for diffusion tensor imaging. AJNR Am J Neuroradiol 2007; 28: 293–298. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Preibisch C, Wallenhorst T, Heidemann R, et al. Comparison of parallel acquisition techniques generalized autocalibrating partially pa