h JK, et al. Diagnostic accuracy of magnetic resonance angiography for internal carotid artery disease: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Stroke 2008; 39: 2237–2248. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Lin FH, Kwong KK, Belliveau JW, et al. Parallel imaging reconstruction using automatic regularization. Magn Reson Med 2004; 51: 559–567. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Michaely HJ, Herrmann KA, Kramer H, et al. High‐resolution renal MRA: comparison of image quality and vessel depiction with different parallel imaging acceleration factors. J Magn Reson Imaging 2006; 24: 95–100. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Forman C, Piccini D, Grimm R, et al. Reduction of respiratory motion artifacts for free‐breathing whole‐heart coronary MRA by weighted iterative reconstruction. Magn Reson Med 2015; 73: 1885–1895. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Greve T, Sollmann N, Hock A, et al. Highly accelerated time-of-flight magnetic resonance angiography using spiral imaging improves conspicuity of intracranial arterial branches while reducing scan time. Euro Radiol 2020; 30: 855–865. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Sartoretti T, Reischauer C, Sartoretti E, et al. Common artefacts encountered on images acquired with combined compressed sensing and SENSE. Insights into Imaging 2018; 9: 1107–1115. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Sharma SD, Fong CL, Tzung BS, et al. Clinical image quality assessment of accelerated magnetic resonance neuroimaging using compressed sensing. Invest Radiol 2013; 48: 638–645. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Okahara M, Kiyosue H, Yamashita M, et al. Diagnostic accuracy of magnetic resonance angiography for cerebral aneurysms in correlation with 3D-digital subtraction angiographic images: a study of 133 aneurysms. Stroke 2002; 33: 1803–1808. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. Brinjikji W, Zhu Y-Q, Lanzino G, et al. Risk factors for growth of intracranial aneurysms: a sy